headings.text:
Controlled mode By default, ArrowKeyStepper manages the current scrollToColumn and scrollToRow values itself (the props of the same name only set their initial values). To manage them yourself, e.g. to also select a cell by clicking it, set isControlled and update the values in response to onScrollToChange. The registered child is scrolled whenever the controlled values change. Click a cell below, then use the arrow keys: import { useState } from "react";
import {
 ArrowKeyStepper,
 Grid,
 type CellRendererParams,
 type ScrollIndices
} from "react-virtualized";

function ArrowKeyStepperControlled({ width }: { width: number }) {
 const [selected, setSelected] = useState<ScrollIndices>({
 scrollToColumn: 0,
 scrollToRow: 0
 });

 const cellRenderer = ({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 rowIndex,
 style
 }: CellRendererParams) => {
 const isSelected =
 columnIndex === selected.scrollToColumn &&
 rowIndex === selected.scrollToRow;

 return (
 <div
 className={[
 "flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-center border-r border-b border-white/10 text-sm",
 isSelected ? "bg-sky-500/40 text-white" : "hover:bg-white/5"
 ].join(" ")}
 key={key}
 onClick={() =>
 setSelected({ scrollToColumn: columnIndex, scrollToRow: rowIndex })
 }
 style={style}
 >
 {`r:${rowIndex}, c:${columnIndex}`}
 </div>
 );
 };

 return (
 <ArrowKeyStepper
 columnCount={100}
 isControlled
 onScrollToChange={setSelected}
 rowCount={100}
 scrollToColumn={selected.scrollToColumn}
 scrollToRow={selected.scrollToRow}
 >
 {({ onSectionRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={100}
 columnWidth={getColumnWidth}
 height={200}
 onSectionRendered={onSectionRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={100}
 rowHeight={getRowHeight}
 width={width}
 />
 )}
 </ArrowKeyStepper>
 );
} Arrow-key stepping can be turned off with the disabled prop. An uncontrolled stepper's current values can also be overridden imperatively, through a ref (see methods below).

mode?: "cells" | "edges" = "edges" Controls behavior of stepper when arrow key direction changes.
"cells" means that the index will only increment or decrement by 1;
"edges" means that the opposite side of the grid will be incremented.

text:
ArrowKeyStepper decorates a virtualized component (such as a Grid, List, or Table) and responds to arrow-key events by scrolling one row or column at a time. This provides a snap-to behavior rather than the default browser scrolling behavior. Click inside the grid below to focus it, then use the up, down, left, and right arrow keys to move around: mode: "edges" (default) mode: "cells" ArrowKeyStepper uses a render function as its children. Pass the onSectionRendered parameter through to the decorated component (it informs the stepper which range of cells is currently visible) and attach the registerChild parameter as its ref; whenever the active cell changes, the stepper scrolls it into view by calling the component's scrollToCell method. The scrollToColumn and scrollToRow parameters identify the active cell, e.g. to highlight it. import { ArrowKeyStepper, Grid } from "react-virtualized";

function ArrowKeyStepperGrid({
 mode,
 width
}: {
 mode: "cells" | "edges";
 width: number;
}) {
 return (
 <ArrowKeyStepper columnCount={100} mode={mode} rowCount={100}>
 {({ onSectionRendered, registerChild, scrollToColumn, scrollToRow }) => (
 <>
 <div className="mb-2 text-sm whitespace-nowrap">
 Most-recently-stepped column: {scrollToColumn}, row: {scrollToRow}
 </div>
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={(params) =>
 renderCell({
 ...params,
 isSelected:
 params.columnIndex === scrollToColumn &&
 params.rowIndex === scrollToRow
 })
 }
 columnCount={100}
 columnWidth={getColumnWidth}
 height={200}
 onSectionRendered={onSectionRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={100}
 rowHeight={getRowHeight}
 width={width}
 />
 </>
 )}
 </ArrowKeyStepper>
 );
} The mode prop controls how the stepper behaves when an arrow key is pressed: in "edges" mode (the default), the next row or column beyond the visible edge of the grid is scrolled into view; in "cells" mode, the current row or column index is only incremented or decremented by 1. Unlike the other HOCs in react-virtualized, ArrowKeyStepper adds a <div> element around its children in order to attach a key-down event handler. The appearance of this wrapper element can be customized using the className property.

headings.text:
Dynamic row heights The most common use case is a List whose rows contain wrapping text. Each row is rendered at the width of the list and its height is measured: Three pieces work together to make this work: A CellMeasurerCache stores the measurements. It's configured with fixedWidth: true since only heights are measured. Each row is wrapped in a CellMeasurer, which is passed the cache along with the row's index and the parent provided to the row renderer. The cache is passed to the List as deferredMeasurementCache, and its rowHeight method is used as the rowHeight prop. import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 List,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 // Rows have a fixed width (the width of the list) and a dynamic height.
 // Unmeasured rows are assumed to be 50px tall.
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultHeight: 50, fixedWidth: true })
 );

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, parent, style }: RowRendererParams) => {
 const item = items[index];

 return (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={0}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 <div
 className="flex flex-col gap-1 px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/10"
 style={style}
 >
 <div className="font-bold text-white">{item.name}</div>
 <div>{item.randomLong}</div>
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 };

 return (
 // Text wraps differently at different widths,
 // so measurements must be cleared when the list resizes
 <AutoSizer
 onResize={() => cache.clearAll()}
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={height}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} CellMeasurer attaches a ref to its child in order to find the DOM element to measure, so the child should be a DOM element (or a component that passes its ref along to one; see child requirements). The style passed to the row (or cell) renderer must still be applied to the element, in order to position it. Measurements depend on the available width when text wraps. When the width changes (e.g. on window resize), call cache.clearAll() so that cells get re-measured, as the examples on this page do in the AutoSizer onResize callback.

Dynamic height Table column A Table can mix fixed and dynamic content. Here only the last column is measured (with a Column cellRenderer); the height of each row is determined by that cell. import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 Column,
 Table
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 // Rows have a dynamic height (but are never shorter than 25px)
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ fixedWidth: true, minHeight: 25 })
 );

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 onResize={() => cache.clearAll()}
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 headerClassName="text-white"
 headerHeight={30}
 height={height}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={125} />
 <Column dataKey="color" label="Color" width={75} />
 {/* Only this column is measured; the others are a single line */}
 <Column
 cellRenderer={({ parent, rowData, rowIndex }) => (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={0}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={rowIndex}
 >
 <div className="py-1 whitespace-normal">
 {rowData.randomLong}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 )}
 dataKey="randomLong"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

Dynamic row heights in a Grid A Grid with fixed-width columns and dynamic row heights; each row is as tall as its tallest cell: import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 Grid,
 type CellRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ items, width }: { items: ListItem[]; width: number }) {
 // Columns have a fixed width (150px) and rows have a dynamic height;
 // each row is as tall as its tallest cell.
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultWidth: 150, fixedWidth: true })
 );

 const cellRenderer = ({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 parent,
 rowIndex,
 style
 }: CellRendererParams) => (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={columnIndex}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={rowIndex}
 >
 <div style={{ ...style, width: 150 }}>
 {items[(rowIndex + columnIndex) % items.length].randomLong}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );

 return (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={50}
 columnWidth={150}
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={400}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

Function children: measure and registerChild Instead of an element, CellMeasurer also accepts a function child. The function receives two parameters: measure: re-measures the cell. Call this when the cell's content is ready, or has changed size, after the initial render (for example when an image finishes loading). Calling it after the cell has unmounted (e.g. it scrolled out of view before the image loaded) does nothing. Both functions are stable. registerChild: specifies the DOM element to measure; it can be used as a ref. A registered element takes precedence over the element returned by the function. This example shows a List of images whose row heights can't be known until the image data has loaded: import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 List,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 height,
 imageUrls,
 width
}: {
 height: number;
 imageUrls: string[];
 width: number;
}) {
 // Average row height is assumed to be about 50px;
 // the width of each row is not dynamic.
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultHeight: 50, fixedWidth: true })
 );

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, parent, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={0}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 {({ measure, registerChild }) => (
 // "style" is required to position the row (within the parent List)
 <div ref={registerChild} style={style}>
 {/* Re-measure the row once the image has loaded */}
 <img onLoad={measure} src={imageUrls[index]} />
 </div>
 )}
 </CellMeasurer>
 );

 return (
 <List
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={height}
 rowCount={imageUrls.length}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

Child requirements CellMeasurer measures the DOM element its ref is attached to. (If the child element has a ref of its own, that ref still receives the element.) The child can be: A DOM element, e.g. <div style={style}>. A function component that passes its ref along to a DOM element. In React 19, ref is a regular prop that can be passed to the element; in React 18, the component must use forwardRef. Anything else (e.g. a component that doesn't pass its ref along, a class component, or a fragment), as long as a function child passes registerChild to the element to be measured. An element registered with registerChild takes precedence over the child element, so it can also be used to measure an element nested within the child. import { forwardRef, useState, type CSSProperties, type Ref } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 List,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

// React 19: "ref" is a regular prop; pass it along to the DOM element
function Row({
 ref,
 style,
 text
}: {
 ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement>;
 style: CSSProperties;
 text: string;
}) {
 return (
 <div ref={ref} style={style}>
 {text}
 </div>
 );
}

// React 18: function components must use forwardRef to receive a ref
const ForwardRefRow = forwardRef<
 HTMLDivElement,
 { style: CSSProperties; text: string }
>(function ForwardRefRow({ style, text }, ref) {
 return (
 <div ref={ref} style={style}>
 {text}
 </div>
 );
});

// Either version: attach registerChild to the element to be measured
function RegisteredRow({
 registerChild,
 style,
 text
}: {
 registerChild: Ref<HTMLDivElement>;
 style: CSSProperties;
 text: string;
}) {
 return (
 <div ref={registerChild} style={style}>
 {text}
 </div>
 );
}

function Example({ items }: { items: string[] }) {
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultHeight: 50, fixedWidth: true })
 );

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, parent, style }: RowRendererParams) => {
 const text = items[index];

 switch (index % 3) {
 case 0:
 return (
 <CellMeasurer
 key={key}
 cache={cache}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 <Row style={style} text={text} />
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 case 1:
 return (
 <CellMeasurer
 key={key}
 cache={cache}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 <ForwardRefRow style={style} text={text} />
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 default:
 return (
 <CellMeasurer
 key={key}
 cache={cache}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 {({ registerChild }) => (
 <RegisteredRow
 registerChild={registerChild}
 style={style}
 text={text}
 />
 )}
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 }
 };

 return (
 <List
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={300}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={300}
 />
 );
} If there is no DOM element to measure (e.g. the child is text, an array or a fragment, or a component that doesn't pass its ref along), the child is rendered as-is, the cell keeps the cache's default size, and a warning is logged in development mode.

keyMapper?: ((rowIndex: number, columnIndex: number) => unknown) Maps a cell's row and column index to a cache key, e.g. an item ID.
This prevents the cached measurements from being invalidated when the parent collection is modified
(e.g. items are inserted or reordered).
Defaults to a mapper that returns `${rowIndex}-${columnIndex}`.
 While all of the parameters above are optional, you should specify either fixedWidth or fixedHeight. CellMeasurerCache is not meant to measure cells that are both dynamic width and height; it would be inefficient to do so since the size of a row (or column) is equal to the largest cell within that row. A warning is logged in development mode if neither is specified. A custom keyMapper can also speed things up when every cell is known to have the same (but not known ahead of time) size: import { CellMeasurerCache } from "react-virtualized";

// Normally every cell is measured individually, which can be slow.
// If all rows are known to have the same (but unknown) height,
// a keyMapper that returns a constant tells CellMeasurer that
// every measurement after the first one will hit the cache.
const cache = new CellMeasurerCache({
 defaultHeight: 30,
 fixedWidth: true,
 keyMapper: () => 1
});

columnWidth Returns the derived width of a column (its widest measured cell) or the default width.
Pass this as the columnWidth prop of a Grid (or MultiGrid);
it's bound to the cache, so it can be passed as-is.
 columnWidth(params: { index: number }): number;

getHeight Returns the (measured or default) height of the specified cell.
columnIndex defaults to 0.
 getHeight(rowIndex: number, columnIndex?: number): number;

getWidth Returns the (measured or default) width of the specified cell.
columnIndex defaults to 0.
 getWidth(rowIndex: number, columnIndex?: number): number;

has Returns true if the specified cell has already been measured.
columnIndex defaults to 0.
 has(rowIndex: number, columnIndex?: number): boolean;

hasFixedHeight Returns true if cells have a fixed height (and a dynamic, measured width);
see the fixedHeight option.
 hasFixedHeight(): boolean;

hasFixedWidth Returns true if cells have a fixed width (and a dynamic, measured height);
see the fixedWidth option.
 hasFixedWidth(): boolean;

rowHeight Returns the derived height of a row (its tallest measured cell) or the default height.
Pass this as the rowHeight prop of a Grid, List or Table;
it's bound to the cache, so it can be passed as-is.
 rowHeight(params: { index: number }): number;

Clearing measurements For example, a row that can be expanded and collapsed should clear its cached measurement when it changes, and tell the List to recompute its layout (see List methods): import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 List,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ messages }: { messages: string[] }) {
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultHeight: 50, fixedWidth: true })
 );
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);
 const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState<Set<number>>(new Set());

 const toggle = (index: number) => {
 setExpanded((prev) => {
 const next = new Set(prev);
 if (next.has(index)) {
 next.delete(index);
 } else {
 next.add(index);
 }
 return next;
 });

 // The row's content (and so its height) is about to change:
 // discard its cached measurement and tell the List to re-layout
 cache.clear(index, 0);
 listRef.current?.recomputeRowHeights(index);
 };

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, parent, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={0}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={index}
 >
 <div onClick={() => toggle(index)} style={style}>
 {expanded.has(index) ? messages[index] : messages[index].slice(0, 25)}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );

 return (
 <List
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={300}
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={messages.length}
 rowHeight={cache.rowHeight}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={300}
 />
 );
}

MultiGrid A MultiGrid (with a fixed first column) whose columns have dynamic widths: import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 MultiGrid,
 type CellRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ items, width }: { items: ListItem[]; width: number }) {
 // Rows have a fixed height (30px) and columns have a dynamic width;
 // each column is as wide as its widest cell.
 const [cache] = useState(
 () =>
 new CellMeasurerCache({
 defaultHeight: 30,
 defaultWidth: 150,
 fixedHeight: true
 })
 );

 const cellRenderer = ({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 parent,
 rowIndex,
 style
 }: CellRendererParams) => (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={columnIndex}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={rowIndex}
 >
 <div style={{ ...style, whiteSpace: "nowrap" }}>
 {items[(rowIndex + columnIndex) % items.length].random}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );

 return (
 <MultiGrid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={50}
 columnWidth={cache.columnWidth}
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 fixedColumnCount={1}
 height={400}
 rowCount={50}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

text:
The approach used for dynamic row heights also works in the other direction. This Grid has fixed row heights and columns whose width is determined by their (non-wrapping) content; the cache is configured with fixedHeight: true and its columnWidth method is used as the columnWidth prop. import { useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 Grid,
 type CellRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 // Cells have a fixed height and a dynamic width.
 // Unmeasured columns are assumed to be 100px wide.
 const [cache] = useState(
 () => new CellMeasurerCache({ defaultWidth: 100, fixedHeight: true })
 );

 const cellRenderer = ({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 parent,
 rowIndex,
 style
 }: CellRendererParams) => {
 const item = items[(rowIndex + columnIndex) % items.length];

 let content;
 switch (columnIndex % 3) {
 case 0:
 content = item.color;
 break;
 case 1:
 content = item.name;
 break;
 default:
 content = item.random;
 break;
 }

 return (
 <CellMeasurer
 cache={cache}
 columnIndex={columnIndex}
 key={key}
 parent={parent}
 rowIndex={rowIndex}
 >
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-3 whitespace-nowrap border-r border-b border-white/10"
 style={{ ...style, height: 35 }}
 >
 {content}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 };

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={1000}
 columnWidth={cache.columnWidth}
 deferredMeasurementCache={cache}
 height={height}
 rowCount={50}
 rowHeight={35}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

ColumnSizer decorates a Grid and calculates the width of its columns based on the available width, the number of columns, and optional minimum and maximum column widths. Change the values below (or resize the window) to see the columns resize: Number of columns Min column width Max column width ColumnSizer uses a render function as its children. It is passed the following parameters: columnWidth: the calculated column width; pass it to the Grid's columnWidth prop registerChild: pass it to the Grid's ref prop, so that column sizes can be recomputed when the ColumnSizer props change adjustedWidth: the lesser of the overall width or the width of all columns; use it as the Grid's width to make the grid shrink to fit sparse content (as in the example above) import { ColumnSizer, Grid } from "react-virtualized";

function ColumnSizerGrid({
 columnCount,
 columnMaxWidth,
 columnMinWidth,
 width
}: {
 columnCount: number;
 columnMaxWidth?: number;
 columnMinWidth?: number;
 width: number;
}) {
 return (
 <ColumnSizer
 columnCount={columnCount}
 columnMaxWidth={columnMaxWidth}
 columnMinWidth={columnMinWidth}
 width={width}
 >
 {({ adjustedWidth, columnWidth, registerChild }) => (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={columnCount}
 columnWidth={columnWidth}
 height={160}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={4}
 rowHeight={40}
 // Shrink to fit if the columns don't fill the available width
 width={adjustedWidth}
 />
 )}
 </ColumnSizer>
 );
} A getColumnWidth function is also passed to the children function. It is a legacy parameter; use columnWidth instead. ColumnSizer needs to know the width of the grid; it is typically used together with AutoSizer.

headings.text:
Rendering cells The cellRenderer function is responsible for rendering a single cell, given its row and column index. It receives the following parameters: import type { CellRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";

function cellRenderer({
 columnIndex, // Horizontal (column) index of cell
 isScrolling, // The Grid is currently being scrolled
 key, // Unique key within array of cells
 rowIndex, // Vertical (row) index of cell
 style // Style object to be applied to cell (to position it)
 // Also available:
 // isVisible: This cell is visible within the grid (eg it is not an overscanned cell)
 // parent: Reference to the parent Grid (instance)
}: CellRendererParams) {
 const user = list[rowIndex][columnIndex];

 // If cell content is complex, consider rendering a lighter-weight placeholder while scrolling
 const content = isScrolling ? "..." : <User user={user} />;

 // Style is required since it specifies how the cell is to be sized and positioned;
 // Grid depends on this sizing/positioning for proper scrolling behavior.
 // By default it contains position, left, top, height, and width properties.
 // You can add class names or style properties as you like (but don't mutate the object).
 // Key is also required by React to more efficiently manage the array of cells.
 return (
 <div key={key} style={style}>
 {content}
 </div>
 );
} The style parameter must be applied to the rendered cell; it specifies how the cell is sized and positioned, and the grid depends on it to scroll correctly. Don't modify the style object (it is cached and re-used by the grid); create a new object if you need to add style properties. Rows and columns don't all need to be the same size; see variable sizes. Grid supports the following static class names, in addition to the className, id, and style props (and containerStyle for the inner container): ReactVirtualized__Grid: main (outer) element ReactVirtualized__Grid__innerScrollContainer: inner scrollable area See customizing styles for more information.

text:
Grid renders tabular data with virtualization along both the vertical and horizontal axes. Only the cells needed to fill the grid (based on its current horizontal and vertical scroll position) are rendered. To render a grid, specify its size (width and height), the number of rows and columns (rowCount and columnCount), the size of each (rowHeight and columnWidth), and a function that renders a cell (cellRenderer). The example above uses AutoSizer to fill the available space: import { Grid, type CellRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

// Each row is an item in the list; each column is one of its fields
const columns = ["index", "name", "color", "size", "random"] as const;

function cellRenderer({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 rowIndex,
 style
}: CellRendererParams) {
 const item = list[rowIndex];
 const field = columns[columnIndex];

 return (
 <div
 className="truncate border-b border-white/10 px-2 leading-[30px]"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {item[field]}
 </div>
 );
}

function GridBasic() {
 return (
 // AutoSizer measures the available space; Grid requires an explicit width and height
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={columns.length}
 columnWidth={150}
 height={height}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} Grid is also the building block for the other components in this library; List, Table, and MultiGrid all render a Grid internally. See Grid props and Grid methods for the full API.

The cellRangeRenderer prop is an advanced property, responsible for rendering the group of cells within a range of row and column indices. It is useful for situations where the grid requires additional, overlaid UI, such as a Gantt chart or a calendar application. Many use cases can be solved more easily using the onScroll callback or the ScrollSync component. If you do want to override cellRangeRenderer, the easiest way is to decorate the defaultCellRangeRenderer function (exported by the library) and add your own elements to the array it returns. Elements are positioned within the grid's inner scrollable container: import {
 defaultCellRangeRenderer,
 Grid,
 type CellRangeRenderer
} from "react-virtualized";

// Highlight a range of cells (e.g. an event in a calendar or a task in a Gantt chart)
const EVENT = { startColumn: 2, stopColumn: 5, startRow: 3, stopRow: 4 };

const cellRangeRenderer: CellRangeRenderer = (params) => {
 const {
 columnSizeAndPositionManager,
 horizontalOffsetAdjustment,
 rowSizeAndPositionManager,
 verticalOffsetAdjustment
 } = params;

 // Render the cells as usual...
 const children = defaultCellRangeRenderer(params);

 // ...then add the overlay, positioned using the same size and position data as the cells
 const start = {
 column: columnSizeAndPositionManager.getSizeAndPositionOfCell(
 EVENT.startColumn
 ),
 row: rowSizeAndPositionManager.getSizeAndPositionOfCell(EVENT.startRow)
 };
 const stop = {
 column: columnSizeAndPositionManager.getSizeAndPositionOfCell(
 EVENT.stopColumn
 ),
 row: rowSizeAndPositionManager.getSizeAndPositionOfCell(EVENT.stopRow)
 };

 children.push(
 <div
 className="pointer-events-none absolute flex items-center justify-center rounded border border-sky-400 bg-sky-400/20 text-sky-100"
 key="overlay"
 style={{
 left: start.column.offset + horizontalOffsetAdjustment,
 top: start.row.offset + verticalOffsetAdjustment,
 width: stop.column.offset + stop.column.size - start.column.offset,
 height: stop.row.offset + stop.row.size - start.row.offset
 }}
 >
 My custom overlay
 </div>
 );

 return children;
};

function GridCellRangeRenderer({ width }: { width: number }) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellRangeRenderer={cellRangeRenderer}
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={50}
 columnWidth={100}
 height={250}
 rowCount={100}
 rowHeight={40}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

MultiGrid decorates Grid and adds fixed columns and/or rows. This is already possible using ScrollSync and two or more Grids, but MultiGrid reduces the boilerplate. Fixed columns Fixed rows import { MultiGrid, type CellRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function cellRenderer({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 rowIndex,
 style
}: CellRendererParams) {
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-center border-r border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {columnIndex}, {rowIndex}
 </div>
 );
}

function Example({
 fixedColumnCount,
 fixedRowCount
}: {
 fixedColumnCount: number;
 fixedRowCount: number;
}) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <MultiGrid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 classNameBottomLeftGrid="bg-white/5 border-r-2 border-white/20"
 classNameTopLeftGrid="bg-white/10 font-bold border-r-2 border-b-2 border-white/20"
 classNameTopRightGrid="bg-white/10 font-bold border-b-2 border-white/20"
 columnCount={50}
 columnWidth={75}
 enableFixedColumnScroll
 enableFixedRowScroll
 fixedColumnCount={fixedColumnCount}
 fixedRowCount={fixedRowCount}
 height={height}
 hideBottomLeftGridScrollbar
 hideTopRightGridScrollbar
 rowCount={100}
 rowHeight={40}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} MultiGrid accepts most of the Grid props. The majority of them (e.g. cellRenderer) are relayed to all of the child Grids, while some (e.g. columnCount and rowCount) are adjusted slightly to support fixed rows and columns. Row and column indices passed to cellRenderer (and to columnWidth and rowHeight functions) are always relative to the full grid, including the fixed rows and columns, and the parent passed to cellRenderer is the MultiGrid itself (so it can be passed to CellMeasurer as-is). A MultiGrid always scrolls itself, so (unlike Grid) it has no scrollElement prop and can't be scrolled by the window. Each of the four inner Grids (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right) can be styled separately using the className*Grid and style*Grid props listed (see MultiGrid props). Use enableFixedColumnScroll and enableFixedRowScroll to allow the user to scroll (e.g. with a mouse wheel) while the cursor is over the fixed column or row. The extra scrollbars this adds can be hidden with hideBottomLeftGridScrollbar and hideTopRightGridScrollbar. MultiGrid also works with CellMeasurer to measure dynamic column widths or row heights. To scroll a MultiGrid, call its scrollToCell or scrollToPosition methods through a ref; the fixed rows and columns are kept in sync with the scrollable section.

headings.text:
forceUpdate Forcefully re-render the Grid (and its visible cells),
e.g. because the data rendered by cellRenderer has changed but the Grid's props have not.
 forceUpdate(): void;

getOffsetForCell Gets offsets for a given cell and alignment (see scrollToCell for the alignment values).
The alignment defaults to "auto"; an index of -1 (the default) refers to the last column or row.
 getOffsetForCell(params?: {
 alignment?: Alignment;
 columnIndex?: number;
 rowIndex?: number;
 }): { scrollLeft: number; scrollTop: number };

handleScrollEvent This method handles a scroll event originating from an external scroll control.
It's an advanced method and should probably not be used unless you're implementing a custom scroll-bar solution.
 handleScrollEvent(params: ScrollPosition): void;

invalidateCellSizeAfterRender Invalidate the size of a cell and recompute visible cells after the current render has been committed.
This is a deferred version of recomputeGridSize() (batching the invalidations of a render);
it is intended for advanced use-cases like CellMeasurer.
 invalidateCellSizeAfterRender(params: CellPosition): void;

scrollToCell Ensure the specified column and/or row is visible (scrolling if necessary).
This method can be used to safely scroll back to a cell that a user has scrolled away from even if it was previously scrolled to.
The align parameter (default "auto") controls the alignment of the cell:
"auto" scrolls the least amount possible to ensure that the specified cell is fully visible
(a cell larger than the Grid is aligned to its start, so that its start is visible);
"start" aligns the cell to the top/left of the Grid and "end" to the bottom/right;
"center" aligns the cell in the middle of the Grid.
This is a one-time scroll: the cell is not kept in view afterwards (e.g. if the Grid's size or its cell sizes change).
Cells that haven't been measured yet (e.g. by CellMeasurer) are positioned using their estimated sizes.
Nothing is scrolled while the Grid's width or height is 0.
The column is ignored for single-column grids (e.g. List, Table).
If the Grid has a scrollElement, the scroll element (e.g. the window) is scrolled vertically instead.
 scrollToCell(params: {
 align?: Alignment;
 columnIndex?: number;
 rowIndex?: number;
 }): void;

Calling methods Grid's methods can be called through a ref (typed as GridImperativeAPI). In version 10, these methods replace the scrollToRow, scrollToColumn, scrollToAlignment, scrollTop and scrollLeft props; see scrolling to a cell: import { useRef } from "react";
import { Grid, type GridImperativeAPI } from "react-virtualized";

function GridMethodsRef({ width }: { width: number }) {
 // A ref provides access to the Grid's imperative methods
 const gridRef = useRef<GridImperativeAPI>(null);

 const scrollToMiddle = () => {
 gridRef.current?.scrollToCell({
 align: "center",
 columnIndex: 50,
 rowIndex: 500
 });
 };

 const scrollToTop = () => {
 gridRef.current?.scrollToPosition({ scrollLeft: 0, scrollTop: 0 });
 };

 return (
 <>
 <div className="mb-2 flex gap-2">
 <button className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME} onClick={scrollToMiddle}>
 Center row 500, column 50
 </button>
 <button className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME} onClick={scrollToTop}>
 Scroll to top
 </button>
 </div>
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={100}
 columnWidth={120}
 height={200}
 ref={gridRef}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={35}
 width={width}
 />
 </>
 );
}

scrollToCell Ensure the specified column and/or row is visible (scrolling if necessary); see Grid#scrollToCell.
Indices include the fixed columns and rows (which are always visible, so they are not scrolled to).
The fixed rows and columns are kept in sync with the scrolled section.
 scrollToCell(params: {
 align?: Alignment;
 columnIndex?: number;
 rowIndex?: number;
 }): void;

Custom overscan behavior Which cells are overscanned is determined by the overscanIndicesGetter prop, an advanced property that calculates the range of cells to render before and after the visible range, along one axis at a time (it is called once for rows and once for columns). By default (defaultOverscanIndicesGetter), cells are only overscanned in the direction the user is scrolling, which is where content will be needed next. The library also exports an accessibilityOverscanIndicesGetter, which always renders at least one extra cell in both directions. This is necessary to support keyboard navigation (Tab / Shift+Tab) between cells in some cases. You can also provide your own function; for example, this one overscans the same number of cells in both directions (it is the "custom" option in the demo above): import type { OverscanIndicesGetter } from "react-virtualized";

// Overscan the same number of cells before and after the visible range,
// regardless of which direction the user is scrolling
const overscanIndicesGetter: OverscanIndicesGetter = ({
 cellCount, // Number of rows or columns in the current axis
 overscanCellsCount, // Maximum number of cells to over-render in either direction
 startIndex, // Begin of range of visible cells
 stopIndex // End of range of visible cells
 // Also available:
 // direction: "horizontal" or "vertical"
 // scrollDirection: 1 (forward) or -1 (backward)
}) => ({
 overscanStartIndex: Math.max(0, startIndex - overscanCellsCount),
 overscanStopIndex: Math.min(cellCount - 1, stopIndex + overscanCellsCount)
}); The function returns an object with the (inclusive) overscanStartIndex and overscanStopIndex of the cells to render. Make sure the returned range stays within 0 and cellCount - 1.

text:
Virtualized components only render the rows and columns that are visible. "Overscanning" means rendering a few additional rows or columns just outside of the visible area, so that they are already in place when the user scrolls to them. This reduces the chance of a user scrolling faster than virtualized content can be rendered, which would briefly show empty space (or cause flickering on certain browsers and devices). See this slide for a visual example of why overscanning exists. Grid (and the components built on top of it, like List and Table) supports two overscan props: overscanRowCount: the number of rows to render above/below the visible rows; defaults to 10 overscanColumnCount: the number of columns to render before/after the visible columns; defaults to 0 By default, extra cells are only rendered in the direction the user is scrolling (e.g. below the visible rows when scrolling down), since that is where content will be needed next. Scroll the grid below to see the range of rendered rows change: defaultOverscanIndicesGetter import { Grid, type RenderedSection } from "react-virtualized";

function OverscanDemo({
 onSectionRendered,
 overscanIndicesGetter,
 overscanRowCount,
 width
}: {
 onSectionRendered: (section: RenderedSection) => void;
 overscanIndicesGetter: OverscanIndicesGetter;
 overscanRowCount: number;
 width: number;
}) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={2}
 columnWidth={width / 2}
 height={200}
 // Reports both the visible and the rendered (overscanned) ranges
 onSectionRendered={onSectionRendered}
 overscanIndicesGetter={overscanIndicesGetter}
 overscanRowCount={overscanRowCount}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

The scrollToCell method ensures that a row and/or column is visible, scrolling the grid if necessary. Call it through a ref to the grid. Enter a row and column index below and click "Scroll": import { useRef, useState, type FormEvent } from "react";
import {
 Grid,
 type Alignment,
 type GridImperativeAPI
} from "react-virtualized";

const COLUMN_COUNT = 1000;
const ROW_COUNT = 1000;

function GridScrollToCell({ width }: { width: number }) {
 const gridRef = useRef<GridImperativeAPI>(null);

 const [align, setAlign] = useState<Alignment>("auto");
 const [columnIndex, setColumnIndex] = useState("50");
 const [rowIndex, setRowIndex] = useState("500");

 const onSubmit = (event: FormEvent) => {
 event.preventDefault();

 gridRef.current?.scrollToCell({
 align,
 columnIndex: parseInt(columnIndex, 10),
 rowIndex: parseInt(rowIndex, 10)
 });
 };

 return (
 <>
 <form className="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-end gap-2" onSubmit={onSubmit}>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Row
 <input
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 max={ROW_COUNT - 1}
 min={0}
 onChange={(event) => setRowIndex(event.target.value)}
 required
 type="number"
 value={rowIndex}
 />
 </label>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Column
 <input
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 max={COLUMN_COUNT - 1}
 min={0}
 onChange={(event) => setColumnIndex(event.target.value)}
 required
 type="number"
 value={columnIndex}
 />
 </label>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Alignment
 <select
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 onChange={(event) => setAlign(event.target.value as Alignment)}
 value={align}
 >
 <option value="auto">auto</option>
 <option value="start">start</option>
 <option value="center">center</option>
 <option value="end">end</option>
 </select>
 </label>
 <button className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME} type="submit">
 Scroll
 </button>
 </form>
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={COLUMN_COUNT}
 columnWidth={100}
 height={250}
 ref={gridRef}
 rowCount={ROW_COUNT}
 rowHeight={40}
 width={width}
 />
 </>
 );
} By default ("auto"), the grid scrolls the least amount possible to make the cell fully visible. Use the align parameter to align the cell to the "start" (top/left), "center", or "end" (bottom/right) of the grid instead. Pass only a rowIndex (or only a columnIndex) to scroll in one direction. scrollToCell is a one-time scroll: the grid doesn't remember the cell, so it isn't kept in view if the grid's size, its cell sizes or its row and column counts change afterwards. To scroll to a cell that is being added, call scrollToCell once the grid has rendered it (e.g. in an effect). Cells that haven't been measured yet (e.g. by CellMeasurer) are positioned using their estimated sizes. Use scrollToPosition to scroll to specific offsets instead. In version 10, the scrollToRow, scrollToColumn, scrollToAlignment, scrollTop and scrollLeft props were replaced by the scrollToCell and scrollToPosition methods. The autoHeight, autoWidth and isScrolling props were removed as well: to scroll a Grid with the window, set its scrollElement prop to "window", in which case scrollToCell and scrollToPosition scroll the window (vertically).

Row heights and column widths must be known ahead of time, but they don't all need to be the same. Instead of a number, rowHeight and columnWidth can be functions that return the size of a row or column given its index. The example below renders 1,000 rows and 1,000 columns with variable column widths and (optionally) variable row heights: Use variable row heights import { Grid, type CellRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";

// Column widths are returned by a function rather than being a fixed number
function getColumnWidth({ index }: { index: number }) {
 switch (index) {
 case 0:
 return 50;
 case 1:
 return 100;
 case 2:
 return 300;
 default:
 return 80;
 }
}

// Row heights can be returned by a function too
function getRowHeight({ index }: { index: number }) {
 return list[index % list.length].size;
}

function cellRenderer({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 rowIndex,
 style
}: CellRendererParams) {
 const item = list[rowIndex % list.length];

 if (columnIndex === 0) {
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-center border-b border-white/10 text-xl text-white"
 key={key}
 // Don't modify the style object; Grid caches and re-uses it
 style={{ ...style, backgroundColor: item.color }}
 >
 {item.name.charAt(0)}
 </div>
 );
 }

 let content;
 switch (columnIndex) {
 case 1:
 content = item.name;
 break;
 case 2:
 content = item.random;
 break;
 default:
 content = `r:${rowIndex}, c:${columnIndex}`;
 break;
 }

 return (
 <div
 className={[
 "flex items-center truncate border-r border-b border-white/10 px-2",
 rowIndex % 2 === 1 ? "bg-white/5" : "",
 columnIndex > 2 ? "justify-center" : ""
 ].join(" ")}
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {content}
 </div>
 );
}

function GridVariableSizes({
 useDynamicRowHeight,
 width
}: {
 useDynamicRowHeight: boolean;
 width: number;
}) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={1000}
 columnWidth={getColumnWidth}
 estimatedColumnSize={80}
 height={300}
 noContentRenderer={() => <div>No cells</div>}
 estimatedRowSize={75}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={useDynamicRowHeight ? getRowHeight : 40}
 width={width}
 />
 );
} When sizes are functions, also specify estimatedRowSize and/or estimatedColumnSize. Cells are only measured as they are rendered, so these estimates are used to calculate the total size of the grid (and the size of its scrollbars) before all of its cells have been measured, which makes scrolling more consistent. Grid has no way of knowing when the data behind a rowHeight or columnWidth function changes. If sizes change, call the recomputeGridSize method. For sizes that depend on the rendered content, see CellMeasurer; to calculate column widths from the available width, see ColumnSizer.

headings.text:
Global CSS Load an additional stylesheet (after the default one) that overrides or adds to the global class names that react-virtualized components render with. For example: /* Loaded after react-virtualized/styles.css */
.ReactVirtualized__Table__headerRow {
 text-transform: none;
 border-bottom: 2px solid #ccc;
}

.ReactVirtualized__Table__row:hover {
 background-color: #f5f5f5;
}

.ReactVirtualized__Table__sortableHeaderIcon {
 fill: #2196f3;
} The following global class names are used: Grid ReactVirtualized__Grid ReactVirtualized__Grid__innerScrollContainer List ReactVirtualized__List Table ReactVirtualized__Table ReactVirtualized__Table__Grid ReactVirtualized__Table__headerRow ReactVirtualized__Table__headerColumn ReactVirtualized__Table__headerTruncatedText ReactVirtualized__Table__sortableHeaderColumn ReactVirtualized__Table__sortableHeaderIcon ReactVirtualized__Table__sortableHeaderIcon--ASC ReactVirtualized__Table__sortableHeaderIcon--DESC ReactVirtualized__Table__row ReactVirtualized__Table__rowColumn Masonry ReactVirtualized__Masonry ReactVirtualized__Masonry__innerScrollContainer

Custom class names Components also accept custom class names, which are added alongside the default ones. For example Table supports className, gridClassName, headerClassName, and rowClassName (which can also be a function of the row index), and Column supports className and headerClassName. These work well with utility CSS frameworks; the table below is styled with Tailwind CSS classes: import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 className="text-sm"
 gridClassName="outline-none"
 headerClassName="text-sky-300"
 headerHeight={30}
 height={height}
 // The header row is passed an index of -1
 rowClassName={({ index }) =>
 index < 0
 ? "border-b-2 border-white/20"
 : index % 2
 ? "hover:bg-white/10"
 : "bg-white/5 hover:bg-white/10"
 }
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={60} />
 <Column
 className="font-bold"
 dataKey="name"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Name"
 width={150}
 />
 <Column
 cellRenderer={({ cellData }) => (
 <span
 className="inline-block w-3 h-3 rounded-full"
 style={{ backgroundColor: cellData }}
 />
 )}
 dataKey="color"
 label="Color"
 style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center" }}
 width={60}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} They also work well with CSS modules: .table {
 font-size: 0.875rem;
}

.headerColumn {
 color: #7dd3fc;
}

.row {
 border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
} import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";
import styles from "./CustomizingStylesCssModules.module.css";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 return (
 <Table
 className={styles.table}
 headerClassName={styles.headerColumn}
 headerHeight={30}
 height={300}
 rowClassName={styles.row}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={400}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={250} />
 <Column dataKey="color" label="Color" width={100} />
 </Table>
 );
}

Inline styles Components also support inline style props for style overrides (e.g. Table supports style, gridStyle, headerStyle, and rowStyle): import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 return (
 <Table
 headerHeight={30}
 headerStyle={{ color: "#7dd3fc" }}
 height={300}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowStyle={({ index }) =>
 index % 2 === 0 ? { backgroundColor: "rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05)" } : {}
 }
 style={{ fontSize: "0.875rem" }}
 width={400}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={250} />
 <Column
 dataKey="color"
 label="Color"
 style={{ fontFamily: "monospace" }}
 width={100}
 />
 </Table>
 );
} See the props pages (e.g. List props, Grid props, Table props, and Column props) to learn which class name and style props each component supports.

text:
The InfiniteLoader component was created to help break large data sets down into chunks that can be just-in-time loaded as they are scrolled into view. It can also be used to create an infinite-loading list (like a social media feed), where the total number of items isn't known up front and more items are loaded as the user reaches the end of the list. The trick is to render one extra row at the end of the list (as long as there are more items to load) to hold a loading indicator. That row is the only one that is not "loaded", so when it scrolls into view (or within InfiniteLoader's threshold), InfiniteLoader asks for the next page to be loaded. Here's a basic example of how you might implement that: import {
 InfiniteLoader,
 List,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function InfiniteLoadingList({
 hasNextPage,
 isNextPageLoading,
 items,
 loadNextPage
}: {
 /** Are there more items to load? (This information comes from the most recent API request.) */
 hasNextPage: boolean;
 /** Are we currently loading a page of items? (This may be an in-flight flag in your store, for example.) */
 isNextPageLoading: boolean;
 /** List of items loaded so far */
 items: ListItem[];
 /** Callback function responsible for loading the next page of items */
 loadNextPage: () => Promise<void>;
}) {
 // If there are more items to be loaded then add an extra row to hold a loading indicator.
 const rowCount = hasNextPage ? items.length + 1 : items.length;

 // Only load 1 page of items at a time.
 // Pass an empty callback to InfiniteLoader in case it asks us to load more than once.
 const loadMoreRows = isNextPageLoading ? () => {} : loadNextPage;

 // Every row is loaded except for our loading indicator row.
 const isRowLoaded = ({ index }: { index: number }) =>
 !hasNextPage || index < items.length;

 // Render a list item or a loading indicator.
 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-4 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {isRowLoaded({ index }) ? (
 items[index].name
 ) : (
 <span className="italic text-slate-500">Loading...</span>
 )}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 )}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 );
} The component above doesn't care where its data comes from. The demo on this page manages it with React state and simulates a network request for each page of items: import { useState } from "react";

const PAGE_SIZE = 25;
const MAX_ITEMS = 250;

function InfiniteLoadingListDemo() {
 const [items, setItems] = useState<ListItem[]>([]);
 const [isNextPageLoading, setIsNextPageLoading] = useState(false);

 const hasNextPage = items.length < MAX_ITEMS;

 const loadNextPage = async () => {
 setIsNextPageLoading(true);

 const page = await fetchPage(items.length, PAGE_SIZE);

 setItems((prevItems) => [...prevItems, ...page]);
 setIsNextPageLoading(false);
 };

 return (
 <InfiniteLoadingList
 hasNextPage={hasNextPage}
 isNextPageLoading={isNextPageLoading}
 items={items}
 loadNextPage={loadNextPage}
 />
 );
} Only one page is loaded at a time: while a page is loading, loadMoreRows is a no-op, in case InfiniteLoader asks for more rows to be loaded more than once.

headings.text:
How it works createTableMultiSort is passed a callback function, which it calls whenever the sort criteria change. It returns an object with three properties: sort: a function to pass to the Table sort prop; it updates the sort criteria in response to header clicks. sortBy: an ordered array of the fields data is sorted by, in order of importance. sortDirection: a map of field name to its sort direction ("ASC" or "DESC"). import { createTableMultiSort } from "react-virtualized";

const sortState = createTableMultiSort(({ sortBy, sortDirection }) => {
 // "sortBy" is an ordered array of fields, e.g. ["lastName", "firstName"]
 // "sortDirection" maps each field to "ASC" or "DESC", e.g. { lastName: "ASC", firstName: "DESC" }
 // Sort your collection however you'd like,
 // then update your component state (or store) to re-render the Table.
 console.log(sortBy, sortDirection);
});

// sortState.sort: pass this to the Table "sort" prop
// sortState.sortBy and sortState.sortDirection: the current sort criteria Because the Table sortBy and sortDirection props only describe a single column, they should be left undefined. Instead, use a custom headerRenderer to show a SortIndicator for each of the sorted columns: import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 createTableMultiSort,
 SortIndicator,
 Table,
 type ColumnProps
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

type SortState = {
 sortBy: string[];
 sortDirection: { [dataKey: string]: "ASC" | "DESC" };
};

function Example() {
 // Sort criteria are stored in React state so that changes re-render the Table
 const [{ sortBy, sortDirection }, setSortState] = useState<SortState>({
 sortBy: ["name"],
 sortDirection: { name: "ASC" }
 });

 // createTableMultiSort tracks the sort criteria;
 // it calls this function (with new sortBy and sortDirection values) whenever they change.
 const [multiSort] = useState(() =>
 createTableMultiSort(
 ({ sortBy, sortDirection }) => setSortState({ sortBy, sortDirection }),
 {
 defaultSortBy: ["name"],
 defaultSortDirection: { name: "ASC" }
 }
 )
 );

 // Sort the collection by each field, in order of importance
 const sortedList = useMemo(
 () =>
 [...list].sort((a, b) => {
 for (const dataKey of sortBy) {
 const key = dataKey as keyof ListItem;
 const order = a[key] < b[key] ? -1 : a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : 0;
 if (order !== 0) {
 return sortDirection[dataKey] === "DESC" ? -order : order;
 }
 }
 return 0;
 }),
 [sortBy, sortDirection]
 );

 // Use the multi-sort state (rather than the Table's sortBy prop) to show indicators
 const headerRenderer: ColumnProps["headerRenderer"] = ({
 dataKey,
 label
 }) => {
 const position = sortBy.indexOf(dataKey);
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center gap-1">
 <span title={typeof label === "string" ? label : undefined}>
 {label}
 </span>
 {position >= 0 && (
 <>
 <SortIndicator sortDirection={sortDirection[dataKey]} />
 {sortBy.length > 1 && (
 <span className="text-xs text-slate-400">{position + 1}</span>
 )}
 </>
 )}
 </div>
 );
 };

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={36}
 height={250}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={sortedList.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => sortedList[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 // Connect the multi-sort state to Table by way of the "sort" prop
 sort={multiSort.sort}
 sortBy={undefined}
 sortDirection={undefined}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column
 dataKey="name"
 headerRenderer={headerRenderer}
 label="Name"
 width={140}
 />
 <Column
 dataKey="size"
 headerRenderer={headerRenderer}
 label="Size"
 width={100}
 />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 headerRenderer={headerRenderer}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} New sortBy and sortDirection values are created each time the sort criteria change (previous values are never mutated), so they can be stored in React state as they are. In version 9, they were mutated in place and had to be copied first.

Default sort criteria createTableMultiSort also accepts default sort-by values. Fields without a default direction are sorted in ascending order. import { createTableMultiSort } from "react-virtualized";

const sortState = createTableMultiSort(sortCallback, {
 defaultSortBy: ["firstName", "lastName"],
 defaultSortDirection: {
 firstName: "ASC",
 lastName: "ASC"
 }
});

text:
By default, Table assumes that sortable rows can be sorted in ascending or descending order. Clicking a column header the first time calls the sort callback with a suggested ordering of ascending. Clicking it a second time suggests descending, and clicking a third time suggests ascending again. This is often desirable, but it has a subtle side effect: once a Table has been sorted, there is no way to return to the unsorted, “natural” order. Fortunately this is easy to implement in your application code. In the table below, clicking a column header a third time returns the rows to their natural order: Rather than always applying the suggested direction, the sort callback checks whether the table was already sorted in descending order by the clicked column. If it was, it clears the sort state (so that sortBy and sortDirection are undefined) and the unsorted list is rendered: import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 SortDirection,
 Table,
 type TableProps
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example() {
 // Start out unsorted, in the list's "natural" order
 const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState<TableProps["sortBy"]>(undefined);
 const [sortDirection, setSortDirection] =
 useState<TableProps["sortDirection"]>(undefined);

 const sortedList = useMemo(() => {
 if (!sortBy) {
 return list; // Natural order
 }

 const key = sortBy as keyof ListItem;
 const sorted = [...list].sort((a, b) =>
 a[key] < b[key] ? -1 : a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : 0
 );

 return sortDirection === SortDirection.DESC ? sorted.reverse() : sorted;
 }, [sortBy, sortDirection]);

 const sort: TableProps["sort"] = (params) => {
 if (sortBy === params.sortBy && sortDirection === SortDirection.DESC) {
 // If the list was sorted DESC by this column,
 // return to the "natural" order rather than switching to ASC.
 setSortBy(undefined);
 setSortDirection(undefined);
 } else {
 setSortBy(params.sortBy);
 setSortDirection(params.sortDirection);
 }
 };

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={36}
 height={250}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={sortedList.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => sortedList[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 sort={sort}
 sortBy={sortBy}
 sortDirection={sortDirection}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={80} />
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={120} />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} See sorting for more about how Table sorting works.

Sometimes it is desirable to display a list in reverse order, with the newest items at the top (like a feed of incoming messages). The simplest way to do this is to add new items to the front of the array (like unshift) instead of the end (like push). The List below has a new item added to it every half second: Pause new items Here is a template for doing this. It also shows how to scroll back to the top of the list (using the scrollToRow method) when new items are added: import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import {
 List,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

const MAX_ITEMS = 100;

function ReverseList({ paused }: { paused: boolean }) {
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);
 const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);

 useEffect(() => {
 if (paused) {
 return;
 }

 const interval = setInterval(() => {
 // Add new items to the front of the list (instead of the end)
 setItems((prevItems) => [createItem(), ...prevItems].slice(0, MAX_ITEMS));
 }, 500);

 return () => clearInterval(interval);
 }, [paused]);

 useEffect(() => {
 // If you want to scroll to the top when items are added, you can do it like this
 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ index: 0 });
 }, [items]);

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => {
 const item = items[index];

 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center gap-4 px-4 overflow-hidden border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 <div
 className="w-8 h-8 shrink-0 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-white"
 style={{ backgroundColor: item.color }}
 >
 {item.name.charAt(0)}
 </div>
 <div className="min-w-0">
 <div className="font-bold">{item.name}</div>
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400 truncate">
 {item.createdAt.toLocaleTimeString()} &middot; {item.random}
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 );
 };

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={60}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

headings.text:
Sizes List, Grid, and Table need a height and width to know how many rows to render. AutoSizer can't measure anything on the server, so the sizes it provides are undefined there (and during hydration). Render with fallback sizes until it has measured the available space; otherwise nothing is rendered on the server: import { List } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

// Sizes to render with on the server (e.g. a typical viewport),
// before AutoSizer has measured the available space
const SERVER_HEIGHT = 600;
const SERVER_WIDTH = 800;

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height = SERVER_HEIGHT, width = SERVER_WIDTH }) => (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 )}
 />
 );
} Once the page is hydrated, AutoSizer measures the available space and the list re-renders at its actual size. A component with a scrollElement uses its height prop the same way: as the height of the viewport until the scroll element has been measured.

Rendering at an initial scroll position To render a list scrolled to a position (e.g. one restored from the URL when the user navigates back), use the initialScrollTop prop (and initialScrollLeft for a Grid or MultiGrid). The rows at that offset are rendered on the server, and the list is scrolled to it when it mounts: import { List } from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer,
 savedScrollTop
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
 // E.g. restored from the URL or from session storage
 savedScrollTop: number;
}) {
 return (
 <List
 height={300}
 // Rendered (on the server too) and scrolled to when the List mounts;
 // changing it later has no effect
 initialScrollTop={savedScrollTop}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={600}
 />
 );
} For example, this list is rendered scrolled to row 500 (an initialScrollTop of 15,000 pixels): The browser can't scroll an element before the page has been hydrated, so until then the server-rendered rows are shifted into view instead. After hydration the list is scrolled to the offset, and the rows stay in place. (Until then the scrollbar is shown at the top; if the user scrolls before the page has been hydrated, the rows they see stay in place as well.) Use initialScrollTop rather than calling scrollToRow in an effect: the effect only runs after hydration, so the page would first show the top of the list and then jump. Changing initialScrollTop after mounting has no effect; use scrollToPosition or scrollToRow to scroll a list that has already been rendered.

Filling the available space In the example below, the list fills its container. Drag the bottom edge of the container to resize it; the list will resize to match. import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div
 className={
 index % 2
 ? "flex items-center px-2"
 : "flex items-center px-2 bg-white/5"
 }
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {items[index].name}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} Grid and Table work the same way: import { Grid, type CellRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

const columns = ["name", "color", "random"] as const;

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 const cellRenderer = ({
 columnIndex,
 key,
 rowIndex,
 style
 }: CellRendererParams) => (
 <div className="truncate px-2 leading-[30px]" key={key} style={style}>
 {items[rowIndex][columns[columnIndex]]}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 // Width and height are undefined until AutoSizer has measured its parent
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={columns.length}
 columnWidth={200}
 height={height}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerClassName="text-white"
 headerHeight={30}
 height={height}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={150} />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

Why are width and height undefined? AutoSizer can't know how much space is available until it has been mounted and has measured its parent, so the sizes are undefined during the first render. react-virtualized components require numbers, so render nothing (or a placeholder) until the values are defined. This can be done inside of a renderProp (as the other examples on this page do) or by passing a ChildComponent: import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import {
 AutoSizer,
 type AutoSizerChildProps
} from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center px-2" key={key} style={style}>
 {items[index].name}
 </div>
 );
}

// Define the child component outside of render so that it isn't re-created
// (use renderProp instead if the list needs access to local state or props)
function SizedList({ height, width }: AutoSizerChildProps) {
 if (height === undefined || width === undefined) {
 // Not measured yet (e.g. the first render, or during server rendering)
 return null;
 }

 return (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 );
}

function Example() {
 return <AutoSizer ChildComponent={SizedList} />;
}

Can I use AutoSizer to manage only width or height (not both)? Yes. Only use the dimension you need, and only wait for that one to be defined. For example, a fixed-height list that grows to fill the available width can be created like so: import { List } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 // Only the width is used; the list has a fixed height
 width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={200}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} A component that is scrolled by the window (with the scrollElement prop) only needs the width, too: its height is that of the window's viewport.

Can I use AutoSizer within a flex container? AutoSizer measures its parent element, so when the space to fill is a flex item, wrap AutoSizer in a div that grows to fill that space. Also set min-height: 0 and min-width: 0 on that div; otherwise flex items can't shrink below the size of their content (the list), so the list would grow but never shrink. import { List } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 return (
 <div style={{ display: "flex", height: "100%" }}>
 {/* Other children... */}
 {/* AutoSizer measures this element; min-width/min-height let it shrink below the list's size */}
 <div style={{ flex: "1 1 auto", minHeight: 0, minWidth: 0 }}>
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} Don't make AutoSizer's parent size itself to its content (e.g. height: auto or width: fit-content); the size it reports would then depend on the size of the list, and nothing would be rendered.

Can I use AutoSizer with other HOCs like InfiniteLoader? Yes. AutoSizer can be used within other react-virtualized HOCs such as InfiniteLoader or ScrollSync like so: import { InfiniteLoader, List } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 isRowLoaded,
 loadMoreRows,
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 isRowLoaded: (params: { index: number }) => boolean;
 loadMoreRows: (params: {
 startIndex: number;
 stopIndex: number;
 }) => Promise<unknown>;
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 return (
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 )}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 );
} See the InfiniteLoader page for a live example of this.

Usage InfiniteLoader uses a render function as its child. That function receives two parameters, which should be passed to the virtualized component it renders: onRowsRendered should be passed as the child's onRowsRendered prop. It informs the loader when the user is scrolling. registerChild should be set as the child's ref. It enables a set of rows to be refreshed once their data has finished loading. The isRowLoaded prop tells the loader which rows have already been loaded, and loadMoreRows is called to load rows that haven't been. loadMoreRows should return a Promise that resolves once the data has finished loading; it is used to determine when to refresh the list with the newly-loaded data. import {
 InfiniteLoader,
 List,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

// This example assumes you have a way to know/load this information
const remoteRowCount = 1000;

const list: string[] = [];

function isRowLoaded({ index }: { index: number }) {
 return !!list[index];
}

async function loadMoreRows({
 startIndex,
 stopIndex
}: {
 startIndex: number;
 stopIndex: number;
}) {
 const response = await fetch(
 `path/to/api?startIndex=${startIndex}&stopIndex=${stopIndex}`
 );
 const names: string[] = await response.json();

 // Store response data in list...
 names.forEach((name, offset) => {
 list[startIndex + offset] = name;
 });
}

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 return (
 <div key={key} style={style}>
 {list[index] ?? "Loading..."}
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 return (
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 rowCount={remoteRowCount}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <List
 height={200}
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={remoteRowCount}
 rowHeight={20}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={300}
 />
 )}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 );
} The number of rows to load at a time can be tuned with the minimumBatchSize prop (e.g. to reduce the number of HTTP requests), and how early rows are pre-fetched with the threshold prop. To build a list that loads more items as the user reaches its end (e.g. a feed), see infinite-loading lists.

Using InfiniteLoader with Grid It is not common to use InfiniteLoader and Grid together but it is possible, by mapping the range of rendered cells to a range of "rows" (one per cell): import {
 Grid,
 InfiniteLoader,
 type GridProps,
 type RenderedSection
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 cellRenderer,
 columnCount,
 isRowLoaded,
 loadMoreRows,
 rowCount
}: {
 cellRenderer: GridProps["cellRenderer"];
 columnCount: number;
 isRowLoaded: (params: { index: number }) => boolean;
 loadMoreRows: (params: {
 startIndex: number;
 stopIndex: number;
 }) => Promise<unknown>;
 rowCount: number;
}) {
 return (
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 rowCount={rowCount * columnCount}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => {
 // Grid cells are mapped to a flat list of "rows" (one per cell)
 const onSectionRendered = ({
 columnStartIndex,
 columnStopIndex,
 rowStartIndex,
 rowStopIndex
 }: RenderedSection) => {
 onRowsRendered({
 startIndex: rowStartIndex * columnCount + columnStartIndex,
 stopIndex: rowStopIndex * columnCount + columnStopIndex
 });
 };

 return (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={columnCount}
 columnWidth={100}
 height={300}
 onSectionRendered={onSectionRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={500}
 />
 );
 }}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 );
}

Edge cases and considerations Tracking loaded (and loading) rows InfiniteLoader is not a stateful component, meaning that it does not keep track of which rows it has requested to be loaded. Your component will need to track this information itself to avoid loading rows multiple times. One way to do this is to use a map to track the status of each row: rows with no entry have never been requested, and rows with an entry (either "loading" or "loaded") can be treated as loaded as far as InfiniteLoader is concerned. The demo at the top of this page does this: import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import {
 InfiniteLoader,
 List,
 type InfiniteLoaderImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

type RowStatus = "loading" | "loaded";

function Example() {
 const infiniteLoaderRef = useRef<InfiniteLoaderImperativeAPI>(null);

 // InfiniteLoader doesn't track which rows have been requested;
 // the application must do this to avoid loading rows more than once.
 const rowStatusMapRef = useRef(new Map<number, RowStatus>());

 // Used to re-render after row statuses change
 const [, setVersion] = useState(0);

 const isRowLoaded = ({ index }: { index: number }) =>
 // Rows that are still loading are treated as loaded (to avoid requesting them again)
 rowStatusMapRef.current.has(index);

 const loadMoreRows = async ({
 startIndex,
 stopIndex
 }: {
 startIndex: number;
 stopIndex: number;
 }) => {
 const rowStatusMap = rowStatusMapRef.current;
 for (let index = startIndex; index <= stopIndex; index++) {
 rowStatusMap.set(index, "loading");
 }
 setVersion((version) => version + 1);

 // Simulate a network request
 await new Promise((resolve) =>
 setTimeout(resolve, 1000 + Math.random() * 2000)
 );

 if (rowStatusMap === rowStatusMapRef.current) {
 for (let index = startIndex; index <= stopIndex; index++) {
 rowStatusMap.set(index, "loaded");
 }
 setVersion((version) => version + 1);
 }
 };

 const clearData = () => {
 rowStatusMapRef.current = new Map();
 setVersion((version) => version + 1);

 // Reset InfiniteLoader's cache and reload the visible rows
 infiniteLoaderRef.current?.resetLoadMoreRowsCache(true);
 };

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-4 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {rowStatusMapRef.current.get(index) === "loaded" ? (
 list[index].name
 ) : (
 <div
 className="h-3 rounded bg-white/10 animate-pulse"
 style={{ width: list[index].size }}
 />
 )}
 </div>
 );

 const statuses = Array.from(rowStatusMapRef.current.values());
 const loadingCount = statuses.filter((status) => status === "loading").length;
 const loadedCount = statuses.length - loadingCount;

 return (
 <div className="h-full flex flex-col gap-2">
 <div className="flex items-center gap-4">
 <button className={buttonClassName} onClick={clearData}>
 Flush cached data
 </button>
 <div className="text-sm text-slate-400">
 {loadingCount} loading, {loadedCount} loaded
 </div>
 </div>
 <div className="grow min-h-0">
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 ref={infiniteLoaderRef}
 rowCount={list.length}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 )}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} Memoization and data changes InfiniteLoader memoizes calls to loadMoreRows in order to avoid multiple calls with the same parameters while a user is scrolling. This can have an unexpected impact if the underlying data changes: InfiniteLoader may not know to call loadMoreRows again because, from its point of view, it already made that call for a given range of rows. (React Virtualized components know nothing about the underlying data, only the number of items in the collection.) To address this, call the resetLoadMoreRowsCache method (through a ref) when the underlying data may have changed, e.g. a filtered list whose search criteria changes. (Alternately, your application can call its own loadMoreRows function again with the most recently requested range.) import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import {
 InfiniteLoader,
 List,
 type InfiniteLoaderImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 isRowLoaded,
 items,
 loadMoreRows,
 rowCount,
 searchText
}: {
 isRowLoaded: (params: { index: number }) => boolean;
 items: ListItem[];
 loadMoreRows: (params: {
 startIndex: number;
 stopIndex: number;
 }) => Promise<unknown>;
 rowCount: number;
 searchText: string;
}) {
 const infiniteLoaderRef = useRef<InfiniteLoaderImperativeAPI>(null);

 // When the search criteria changes, previously loaded rows are no longer valid.
 // Clear InfiniteLoader's cache and reload the most recently requested batch of rows.
 useEffect(() => {
 infiniteLoaderRef.current?.resetLoadMoreRowsCache(true);
 }, [searchText]);

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div key={key} style={style}>
 {items[index]?.name ?? "Loading..."}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <InfiniteLoader
 isRowLoaded={isRowLoaded}
 loadMoreRows={loadMoreRows}
 ref={infiniteLoaderRef}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 >
 {({ onRowsRendered, registerChild }) => (
 <List
 height={200}
 onRowsRendered={onRowsRendered}
 ref={registerChild}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={20}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={300}
 />
 )}
 </InfiniteLoader>
 );
}

Empty lists Use the noRowsRenderer prop to render placeholder content when rowCount is 0: import { List } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function noRowsRenderer() {
 return (
 <div className="h-full flex items-center justify-center text-slate-500">
 No rows
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 noRowsRenderer={noRowsRenderer}
 rowCount={0}
 rowHeight={35}
 rowRenderer={() => null}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

text:
List renders a windowed (or "virtualized") list of rows, meaning that only the rows visible within the list (plus a few overscanned rows) are rendered. This keeps the number of DOM elements small, no matter how many rows the list contains. To render a list, specify its size (width and height), how many rows it contains (rowCount), the height of each row (rowHeight), and a function that renders a row (rowRenderer; see rendering rows). Rows are rendered through a function, rather than as children, for performance reasons. The example above uses AutoSizer to fill the available space: import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

// List data as an array of objects, e.g.
// [{ color: "#f44336", name: "Brian Vaughn" }, ...]
const list = LIST;

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 const item = list[index];

 // Rows must apply the "style" parameter (it positions and sizes the row)
 // and the "key" parameter (it identifies the row to React).
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center gap-4 px-4 overflow-hidden border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 <div
 className="w-8 h-8 shrink-0 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-white"
 style={{ backgroundColor: item.color }}
 >
 {item.name.charAt(0)}
 </div>
 <div>
 <div className="font-bold">{item.name}</div>
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400">This is row {index}</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={50}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} List uses a Grid internally to render its rows, and all props are relayed to that inner Grid. That means List also accepts Grid props in addition to its own List props. By default List renders 10 extra rows above and below the visible ones (overscanRowCount), which can help reduce flickering during scrolling on certain browsers and devices. See overscan for an important note about this property. The outer element of a list has the static class name ReactVirtualized__List; a custom className, id, and style can be specified too. See customizing styles for more information.

headings.text:
getOffsetForRow Gets the vertical offset (scrollTop) for a given row and alignment (see scrollToRow for the align values).
The alignment defaults to "auto"; an index of -1 (the default) refers to the last row.
alignment is a deprecated alias of align (align takes precedence if both are specified).
 getOffsetForRow(params?: {
 align?: Alignment;
 /** @deprecated Use `align` instead. */
 alignment?: Alignment;
 index?: number;
 }): number;

invalidateCellSizeAfterRender Invalidate the size of a row (after the current render has been committed); see Grid#invalidateCellSizeAfterRender.
This method is intended for CellMeasurer.
 invalidateCellSizeAfterRender(params: CellPosition): void;

recomputeGridSize Recompute row heights and offsets after the specified row index (defaults to 0); see Grid#recomputeGridSize.
(The column index is ignored.) This method is intended for CellMeasurer; also see recomputeRowHeights.
 recomputeGridSize(params?: Partial<CellPosition>): void;

scrollToCell Ensure the specified row is visible (scrolling if necessary); the column index is ignored.
This allows a List to be used with ArrowKeyStepper; also see scrollToRow.
 scrollToCell(params: {
 align?: Alignment;
 columnIndex?: number;
 rowIndex?: number;
 }): void;

Calling methods List's methods can be called through a ref. The example below uses scrollToRow to scroll the list. In version 10, these methods replace the scrollToIndex, scrollToAlignment and scrollTop props; see scrolling to a row: import { useRef } from "react";
import {
 List,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-between px-4 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {list[index].name}
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-500">Row {index}</div>
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);

 return (
 <div className="h-full flex flex-col gap-2">
 <div className="flex gap-2">
 <button
 className={buttonClassName}
 onClick={() => listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ index: 0 })}
 >
 Scroll to first row
 </button>
 <button
 className={buttonClassName}
 onClick={() =>
 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ align: "center", index: 500 })
 }
 >
 Center row 500
 </button>
 <button
 className={buttonClassName}
 onClick={() =>
 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ index: list.length - 1 })
 }
 >
 Scroll to last row
 </button>
 </div>
 <div className="grow">
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={35}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} To compute the offset of a row without scrolling to it, use getOffsetForRow; it accepts the same align parameter as scrollToRow (e.g. getOffsetForRow({ align: "center", index: 500 })). The alignment parameter of earlier versions is deprecated but still supported.

Recomputing row heights When a rowHeight function is used and the data behind it changes, call recomputeRowHeights so that the list re-measures its rows: import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import {
 List,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);

 // List only knows about the rowHeight function, not the data behind it,
 // so tell it to re-measure rows whenever the underlying data changes.
 useEffect(() => {
 listRef.current?.recomputeRowHeights();
 }, [items]);

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div key={key} style={style}>
 {items[index].name}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <List
 height={300}
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={({ index }) => items[index].size}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={300}
 />
 );
}

Scrolling placeholders If row content is expensive to render, consider rendering a light-weight placeholder while the list is being scrolled. The isScrolling parameter passed to rowRenderer makes this possible: import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function rowRenderer({ index, isScrolling, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 if (isScrolling) {
 // Render a light-weight placeholder while the list is being scrolled
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-4 border-b border-white/10 italic text-slate-500"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 Scrolling...
 </div>
 );
 }

 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-4 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {list[index].name}
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={35}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

text:
The rowRenderer function is responsible for rendering a single row, given its index. It must return an element that applies the style parameter (which positions and sizes the row) and has a unique key, and whose content fits within the row's height: import { type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";

// The following named parameters are passed to the row renderer:
// index: Index of row
// isScrolling: The List is currently being scrolled
// isVisible: This row is visible within the List (eg it is not an overscanned row)
// key: Unique key within array of rendered rows
// parent: Reference to the parent List (can be passed to CellMeasurer)
// style: Style object to be applied to row (to position it)
function rowRenderer({ index, isScrolling, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 const user = list[index];

 // If row content is complex, consider rendering a light-weight placeholder while scrolling.
 const content = isScrolling ? "..." : user.name;

 // Style is required since it specifies how the row is to be sized and positioned.
 // React Virtualized depends on this sizing/positioning for proper scrolling behavior.
 // By default, List provides the following style properties:
 // position, left, top, height, width
 // You can add additional class names or style properties as you would like.
 // Key is also required by React to more efficiently manage the array of rows.
 return (
 <div key={key} style={style}>
 {content}
 </div>
 );
} It is very important that rows do not have vertical overflow. Overflow would make scrolling the list difficult (as individual rows would intercept the scroll events). For this reason it is recommended that rows use a style like overflow-y: hidden.

The scrollToRow method ensures that a row is visible, scrolling the list if necessary. Call it through a ref to the list. By default ("auto") the list scrolls the least amount possible to make the row fully visible (a row taller than the list is scrolled to its start); use the align parameter to align the row to the "start", "center", or "end" of the list instead. import { useRef, useState, type FormEvent } from "react";
import {
 List,
 type Alignment,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-between px-4 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {list[index].name}
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-500">Row {index}</div>
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);

 const [align, setAlign] = useState<Alignment>("auto");
 const [index, setIndex] = useState("500");

 const onSubmit = (event: FormEvent) => {
 event.preventDefault();

 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ align, index: parseInt(index, 10) });
 };

 return (
 <div className="h-full flex flex-col gap-2">
 <form className="flex flex-wrap items-end gap-2" onSubmit={onSubmit}>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Row
 <input
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 max={list.length - 1}
 min={0}
 onChange={(event) => setIndex(event.target.value)}
 required
 type="number"
 value={index}
 />
 </label>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Alignment
 <select
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 onChange={(event) => setAlign(event.target.value as Alignment)}
 value={align}
 >
 <option value="auto">auto</option>
 <option value="start">start</option>
 <option value="center">center</option>
 <option value="end">end</option>
 </select>
 </label>
 <button className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME} type="submit">
 Scroll
 </button>
 </form>
 <div className="grow">
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 height={height}
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={35}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} scrollToRow is a one-time scroll: the list doesn't remember the row, so it isn't kept in view if the list's size, its row heights or its row count change afterwards. To scroll to a row that is being added, call scrollToRow once the list has rendered it (e.g. in an effect). Rows that haven't been measured yet (e.g. by CellMeasurer) are positioned using their estimated heights. In version 10, the scrollToIndex, scrollToAlignment and scrollTop props were replaced by the scrollToRow and scrollToPosition methods, and the autoHeight and isScrolling props were removed: to scroll a List with the window, set its scrollElement prop to "window", in which case scrollToRow scrolls the window. The same applies to Table.

Rows don't all need to be the same height. Instead of a number, rowHeight can be a function that returns the height of a row given its index. In that case, also specify an estimatedRowSize; it is used to estimate the total height of the list before all of its rows have been measured, which makes scrolling more consistent. import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

// Each item specifies its own height (50, 75, or 100 pixels)
const list = LIST;

function rowHeight({ index }: { index: number }) {
 return list[index].size;
}

function rowRenderer({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) {
 const item = list[index];

 return (
 <div
 className="flex items-center gap-4 px-4 overflow-hidden border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 <div
 className="w-8 h-8 shrink-0 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-white"
 style={{ backgroundColor: item.color }}
 >
 {item.name.charAt(0)}
 </div>
 <div className="grow">
 <div className="font-bold">{item.name}</div>
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400">This is row {index}</div>
 {item.size >= 75 && (
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400">It is a taller row.</div>
 )}
 {item.size >= 100 && (
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400">It has a 4th line.</div>
 )}
 </div>
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-500">{item.size}px</div>
 </div>
 );
}

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 estimatedRowSize={75}
 height={height}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowHeight={rowHeight}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} List has no way of knowing when the data behind a rowHeight function changes. If row heights change, call the recomputeRowHeights method. For row heights that depend on the rendered content, see CellMeasurer.

Masonry efficiently displays dynamically-sized, user-positioned cells using windowing techniques. Cells are measured just-in-time and laid out as the user scrolls. Sizes are cached so that resizing (and re-flowing) the layout is fast and does not require re-measuring. Cell positions are controlled by an injected cellPositioner prop. Windowing is vertical; Masonry does not support horizontal scrolling. 200px columns 10px gutter A Masonry requires a CellMeasurerCache (to measure and cache the size of each cell), a cell positioner (to lay out the measured cells), and a cellRenderer that wraps each cell in a CellMeasurer: import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import {
 CellMeasurer,
 CellMeasurerCache,
 createMasonryCellPositioner,
 Masonry,
 type MasonryImperativeAPI
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 columnWidth,
 gutterSize,
 items
}: {
 columnWidth: number;
 gutterSize: number;
 items: ListItem[];
}) {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ height, width }) =>
 height !== undefined && width !== undefined ? (
 <MasonryLayout
 columnWidth={columnWidth}
 gutterSize={gutterSize}
 height={height}
 items={items}
 // Changing the column width changes how text wraps (and so the height of each cell);
 // the simplest way to handle that is to start over with a fresh cache and positioner
 key={columnWidth}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

function MasonryLayout({
 columnWidth,
 gutterSize,
 height,
 items,
 width
}: {
 columnWidth: number;
 gutterSize: number;
 height: number;
 items: ListItem[];
 width: number;
}) {
 const columnCount = Math.max(
 1,
 Math.floor((width + gutterSize) / (columnWidth + gutterSize))
 );

 // Default sizes help Masonry decide how many cells to batch-measure
 const [cache] = useState(
 () =>
 new CellMeasurerCache({
 defaultHeight: 250,
 defaultWidth: columnWidth,
 fixedWidth: true
 })
 );

 // The built-in positioner places each cell in the shortest column
 const [cellPositioner] = useState(() =>
 createMasonryCellPositioner({
 cellMeasurerCache: cache,
 columnCount,
 columnWidth,
 spacer: gutterSize
 })
 );

 const masonryRef = useRef<MasonryImperativeAPI>(null);

 // When the number of columns (or the space between them) changes,
 // update the positioner and re-position the (already measured) cells
 useLayoutEffect(() => {
 cellPositioner.reset({ columnCount, columnWidth, spacer: gutterSize });
 masonryRef.current?.recomputeCellPositions();
 }, [cellPositioner, columnCount, columnWidth, gutterSize]);

 return (
 <Masonry
 cellCount={items.length}
 cellMeasurerCache={cache}
 cellPositioner={cellPositioner}
 cellRenderer={({ index, key, parent, style }) => {
 const item = items[index];

 return (
 <CellMeasurer cache={cache} index={index} key={key} parent={parent}>
 <div
 className="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-2 text-sm"
 style={{ ...style, width: columnWidth }}
 >
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-center rounded-md text-xl text-white"
 style={{ backgroundColor: item.color, height: item.size * 2 }}
 >
 {index}
 </div>
 {item.random}
 </div>
 </CellMeasurer>
 );
 }}
 height={height}
 ref={masonryRef}
 width={width}
 />
 );
} The style passed to cellRenderer positions the cell, so it must be passed through to the rendered element. The parent parameter is the Masonry's imperative handle and should be passed to CellMeasurer. See the props page for the full list of supported props, and measuring and layout for how (and with which constraints) cells are measured and positioned. Masonry renders the following static class names, which can be used for styling: ReactVirtualized__Masonry: main (outer) element ReactVirtualized__Masonry__innerScrollContainer: inner scrollable area

headings.text:
createMasonryCellPositioner Masonry provides a built-in positioner, createMasonryCellPositioner, for a simple column layout. It places each cell into the column that is currently the shortest. It requires a few configuration settings: import {
 CellMeasurerCache,
 createMasonryCellPositioner
} from "react-virtualized";

const cache = new CellMeasurerCache({
 defaultHeight: 250,
 defaultWidth: 200,
 fixedWidth: true
});

const cellPositioner = createMasonryCellPositioner({
 // Contains cell measurements (eg item height); required
 cellMeasurerCache: cache,

 // Number of columns to use in layout; required
 columnCount: 3,

 // Width of each column; required
 columnWidth: 200,

 // Empty space between columns (and between cells within a column);
 // defaults to 0
 spacer: 10
});

Updating the layout If any of these settings change due to external changes (eg a window resize event), update them using the positioner's reset method and then tell Masonry to re-position its cells using recomputeCellPositions() : // eg in response to a window resize
function onResize({ width }: { width: number }) {
 const columnWidth = 250;
 const spacer = 15;

 cellPositioner.reset({
 columnCount: Math.floor((width + spacer) / (columnWidth + spacer)),
 columnWidth,
 spacer
 });

 // Discard cached positions and re-position cells using their cached sizes
 masonryRef.current?.recomputeCellPositions();
} Unlike createMasonryCellPositioner, reset does not default spacer to 0; always specify it.

Custom positioners Custom layouts can be implemented with a custom positioner. Because the positioner tracks layout state as it goes (eg the height of each column), it needs a way to be reset before Masonry re-positions its cells: // Places cells in columns round-robin (left to right, in index order)
// rather than always choosing the shortest column
function createRoundRobinPositioner(columnCount: number, columnWidth: number) {
 let columnHeights = new Array<number>(columnCount).fill(0);

 function cellPositioner(index: number) {
 const column = index % columnCount;
 const left = column * columnWidth;
 const top = columnHeights[column];

 // Sizes have already been measured (and cached) by the time a cell is positioned
 columnHeights[column] = top + cache.getHeight(index);

 return { left, top };
 }

 // recomputeCellPositions() asks for every cell's position again (starting at index 0)
 // so the positioner's internal state must be reset before calling it
 cellPositioner.reset = () => {
 columnHeights = new Array<number>(columnCount).fill(0);
 };

 return cellPositioner;
}

clearCellPositions Clears the internal position cache and re-renders.
Cells are positioned again (by the cellPositioner) as they are rendered.
 clearCellPositions(): void;

invalidateCellSizeAfterRender Notifies the Masonry that the specified cell has been measured for the first time (while rendering);
the cell is positioned (by the cellPositioner) once the current render has been committed, then the Masonry re-renders.
Only rowIndex (the cell's index) is used.
This method is intended for CellMeasurer.
 invalidateCellSizeAfterRender(params: MasonryCellPosition): void;

Usage Masonry exposes these methods through an imperative handle, which can be accessed using a ref (eg useRef<MasonryImperativeAPI>(null)). The cell positioning methods are typically called after updating the cell positioner, eg when the container is resized: // eg in response to a window resize
function onResize({ width }: { width: number }) {
 const columnWidth = 250;
 const spacer = 15;

 cellPositioner.reset({
 columnCount: Math.floor((width + spacer) / (columnWidth + spacer)),
 columnWidth,
 spacer
 });

 // Discard cached positions and re-position cells using their cached sizes
 masonryRef.current?.recomputeCellPositions();
} recomputeCellPositions() synchronously re-positions every cell that has already been measured, using cached measurements. Use it when the layout changes but cell sizes don't (eg the number of columns or the space between them). clearCellPositions() discards cached positions; cells are positioned again only as they are measured. If cell sizes have changed too (eg the column width changed, so text wraps differently), clear the CellMeasurerCache (cache.clearAll()) before calling it, otherwise cells that were already measured won't be re-positioned. invalidateCellSizeAfterRender and recomputeGridSize are called by CellMeasurer (through the parent passed to cellRenderer, which is this handle) and by InfiniteLoader (to re-render cells once they have loaded); they don't need to be called directly.

text:
ScrollSync simplifies the process of synchronizing scrolling between two or more virtualized components. The example below uses three Grids to mimic a spreadsheet with a fixed header row and a fixed first column. Scroll the body of the spreadsheet; the header and first column follow along. It also shows how the scroll values can be used to control other UI, such as background colors. ScrollSync uses a render function as its children. Use the registerChild parameter to register each virtualized component that should be kept in sync (e.g. <Grid ref={registerChild("header")} />), and pass the onScroll parameter to the component(s) the user scrolls. Whenever a scroll offset is reported through onScroll, ScrollSync scrolls the other registered components to match it (only the axes that changed are synced) and calls the render function again with the updated values. The render function receives: registerChild(key): returns a ref callback that registers a component (such as a Grid) under a unique key onScroll: pass this to the scrollable component(s) scrollLeft, scrollTop: the current scroll offsets (e.g. for custom rendering) clientHeight, clientWidth: the size of the visible portion of the scrolled component scrollHeight, scrollWidth: the total size of all rows and columns in the scrolled component Version 10 removed the scrollLeft and scrollTop props from Grid; register the components with registerChild instead of passing the scroll offsets to them. import scrollbarSize from "dom-helpers/scrollbarSize";
import { Grid, ScrollSync } from "react-virtualized";

const COLUMN_COUNT = 50;
const COLUMN_WIDTH = 75;
const HEIGHT = 300;
const ROW_COUNT = 100;
const ROW_HEIGHT = 40;

function ScrollSyncSpreadsheet({ width }: { width: number }) {
 const bodyWidth = width - COLUMN_WIDTH;

 return (
 <ScrollSync>
 {({
 clientHeight,
 clientWidth,
 onScroll,
 registerChild,
 scrollHeight,
 scrollLeft,
 scrollTop,
 scrollWidth
 }) => {
 // Scroll values can also be used to drive other UI, such as these background colors
 const x = scrollLeft / Math.max(1, scrollWidth - clientWidth);
 const y = scrollTop / Math.max(1, scrollHeight - clientHeight);

 return (
 <div className="flex">
 <div style={{ width: COLUMN_WIDTH }}>
 {/* Fixed top-left cell */}
 <div
 className="flex items-center justify-center border-r border-b border-white/10"
 style={{ height: ROW_HEIGHT }}
 />
 {/* Fixed first column; ScrollSync scrolls it vertically with the body */}
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={leftCellRenderer}
 className="overflow-hidden! shadow-[inset_-1px_0_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.1)]"
 columnCount={1}
 columnWidth={COLUMN_WIDTH}
 height={HEIGHT - scrollbarSize()}
 ref={registerChild("left")}
 rowCount={ROW_COUNT}
 rowHeight={ROW_HEIGHT}
 style={{ backgroundColor: `rgb(188 57 89 / ${0.2 + y * 0.6})` }}
 tabIndex={null}
 width={COLUMN_WIDTH}
 />
 </div>
 <div>
 {/* Fixed header row; ScrollSync scrolls it horizontally with the body */}
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={headerCellRenderer}
 className="overflow-hidden! shadow-[inset_0_-1px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.1)]"
 columnCount={COLUMN_COUNT}
 columnWidth={COLUMN_WIDTH}
 height={ROW_HEIGHT}
 ref={registerChild("header")}
 rowCount={1}
 rowHeight={ROW_HEIGHT}
 style={{ backgroundColor: `rgb(71 16 97 / ${0.2 + x * 0.6})` }}
 tabIndex={null}
 width={bodyWidth - scrollbarSize()}
 />
 {/* Scrollable body; its onScroll drives the other two grids */}
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={bodyCellRenderer}
 columnCount={COLUMN_COUNT}
 columnWidth={COLUMN_WIDTH}
 height={HEIGHT}
 onScroll={onScroll}
 ref={registerChild("body")}
 rowCount={ROW_COUNT}
 rowHeight={ROW_HEIGHT}
 width={bodyWidth}
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
 }}
 </ScrollSync>
 );
} For the common case of fixed header rows and/or columns, also take a look at MultiGrid, which handles this layout for you.

Table renders tabular data with a fixed header and windowed rows, for improved performance with large data sets. Its layout is created with flexboxes, and it uses a Grid internally to render only the rows that are visible. A table requires an explicit width and height, the height of its header (headerHeight) and rows (rowHeight), the number of rows (rowCount), and a rowGetter function that returns the data for a given row index. Its children are one or more Columns, each describing a column's header and the row attribute (dataKey) its cells display. import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

// Table data is an array of objects, e.g.
// const list = [{ name: "Brian Vaughn", random: "Software engineer" }, ...];

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={30}
 height={250}
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => list[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={150} />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} The default table styles are in react-virtualized/styles.css; they only need to be imported once. The customizing styles guide explains how to override them. The example above uses AutoSizer to fill the available width. Table content can scroll vertically, but it is not meant to scroll horizontally. Columns shrink and grow to fit the table's width; consider using a MultiGrid for data with many columns.

Each Column accepts three functions that control what its cells and header render: cellDataGetter returns a cell's data, given its dataKey, rowData, and columnData. The default (defaultTableCellDataGetter) reads rowData[dataKey] (or calls rowData.get(dataKey) for Map-like objects). Override it if your data is calculated or requires custom processing. cellRenderer renders a cell's contents, given its cellData, columnData, columnIndex, dataKey, isScrolling, parent, rowData, and rowIndex. The default (defaultTableCellRenderer) displays the cell data as a string. Override it if your data is some other type of object or requires custom formatting. headerRenderer renders the column header, given its columnData, dataKey, disableSort, label, sortBy, and sortDirection. The default (defaultTableHeaderRenderer) displays the label along with a sort indicator if the table is sorted by this column. import { Column, defaultTableHeaderRenderer, Table } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example() {
 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={36}
 height={250}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => list[index]}
 rowHeight={40}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column
 // Render a custom cell (a colored badge)
 cellRenderer={({ cellData, rowData }) => (
 <div
 className="w-6 h-6 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold text-white"
 style={{ backgroundColor: rowData.color }}
 >
 {cellData}
 </div>
 )}
 // Derive the cell's data from the row (rather than reading rowData[dataKey])
 cellDataGetter={({ rowData }) => rowData.name.charAt(0)}
 dataKey="initial"
 label=""
 width={30}
 />
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={120} />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 // Decorate the default header (label and sort indicator)
 headerRenderer={(props) => (
 <span className="text-sky-300">
 {defaultTableHeaderRenderer(props)}
 </span>
 )}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} The columnData prop can be used to pass additional data (e.g. action creators or relational data) to all three of these functions.

Describes the header and cell contents of a table column.
 This component does not render anything itself;
Table reads the props of its children to render headers and cells
(applying defaultColumnProps to each child's props).
Components that render a Column (e.g. const MyColumn = (props) => <Column {...props} />) can be used as Table children too;
their props are read the same way (the wrapper component itself is never rendered).

headings.text:
getOffsetForRow Gets offset for a given row and alignment (align; alignment is a deprecated alias).
The alignment defaults to "auto"; an index of -1 (the default) refers to the last row.
 getOffsetForRow(params?: {
 align?: Alignment;
 /** @deprecated Use `align` instead. */
 alignment?: Alignment;
 index?: number;
 }): number;

invalidateCellSizeAfterRender Invalidate the size of a row (after the current render has been committed); see Grid#invalidateCellSizeAfterRender.
This method is intended for CellMeasurer.
 invalidateCellSizeAfterRender(params: CellPosition): void;

recomputeGridSize Recompute row heights and offsets after the specified row index (defaults to 0); see Grid#recomputeGridSize.
(The column index is ignored.) This method is intended for CellMeasurer; also see recomputeRowHeights.
 recomputeGridSize(params?: Partial<CellPosition>): void;

scrollToCell Ensure the specified row is visible (scrolling if necessary); the column index is ignored.
This allows a Table to be used with ArrowKeyStepper; also see scrollToRow.
 scrollToCell(params: {
 align?: Alignment;
 columnIndex?: number;
 rowIndex?: number;
 }): void;

Calling methods Table's methods can be called through a ref. The example below uses scrollToRow (with an align parameter) and scrollToPosition. As with List, these are one-time scrolls (the row isn't kept in view if the table's size or row heights change afterwards). In version 10, these methods replace the scrollToIndex, scrollToAlignment and scrollTop props. import { useRef, useState, type FormEvent } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 Table,
 type Alignment,
 type TableImperativeAPI
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example() {
 const tableRef = useRef<TableImperativeAPI>(null);

 const [align, setAlign] = useState<Alignment>("center");
 const [index, setIndex] = useState("500");

 const onSubmit = (event: FormEvent) => {
 event.preventDefault();

 tableRef.current?.scrollToRow({ align, index: parseInt(index, 10) });
 };

 const scrollToTop = () => {
 tableRef.current?.scrollToPosition({ scrollTop: 0 });
 };

 return (
 <div className="flex flex-col gap-2">
 <form className="flex flex-wrap items-end gap-2" onSubmit={onSubmit}>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Row
 <input
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 max={list.length - 1}
 min={0}
 onChange={(event) => setIndex(event.target.value)}
 required
 type="number"
 value={index}
 />
 </label>
 <label className={LABEL_CLASS_NAME}>
 Alignment
 <select
 className={INPUT_CLASS_NAME}
 onChange={(event) => setAlign(event.target.value as Alignment)}
 value={align}
 >
 <option value="auto">auto</option>
 <option value="start">start</option>
 <option value="center">center</option>
 <option value="end">end</option>
 </select>
 </label>
 <button className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME} type="submit">
 Scroll
 </button>
 <button
 className={BUTTON_CLASS_NAME}
 onClick={scrollToTop}
 type="button"
 >
 Scroll to top
 </button>
 </form>
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={30}
 height={200}
 ref={tableRef}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => list[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={60} />
 <Column dataKey="name" flexGrow={1} label="Name" width={120} />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 );
}

text:
For more control than rowClassName and rowStyle offer, Table accepts a rowRenderer and a headerRowRenderer. These are advanced properties, useful when you need additional hooks into Table (e.g. integrating with a drag-and-drop library like react-sortable-hoc) or need to render additional UI elements. The easiest way to override them is to decorate the default implementations, defaultTableRowRenderer and defaultTableHeaderRowRenderer, or to fork them. Click a row below to select it: import { useState } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 defaultTableRowRenderer,
 Table,
 type TableProps
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example() {
 const [selectedIndex, setSelectedIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);

 // Decorate the default row renderer rather than re-implementing it;
 // it takes care of event handlers and ARIA attributes.
 const rowRenderer: TableProps["rowRenderer"] = (props) =>
 defaultTableRowRenderer({
 ...props,
 className:
 props.index === selectedIndex
 ? `${props.className} bg-sky-500/30`
 : props.className
 });

 // The header row can be customized in the same way
 const headerRowRenderer: TableProps["headerRowRenderer"] = ({
 className,
 columns,
 style
 }) => (
 <div
 className={`${className} bg-sky-950 text-sky-200`}
 role="row"
 style={style}
 >
 {columns}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={36}
 headerRowRenderer={headerRowRenderer}
 height={250}
 onRowClick={({ index }) => setSelectedIndex(index)}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10 cursor-pointer"
 rowCount={list.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => list[index]}
 rowHeight={36}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={60} />
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={120} />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} A headerRowRenderer is passed the header row className, style, and the rendered columns. A rowRenderer is passed the same values plus the row index, key, rowData, isScrolling, and the onRowClick, onRowDoubleClick, onRowMouseOut, onRowMouseOver, and onRowRightClick handlers. A custom row renderer must apply the style it is passed to the row element; it positions the row within the table.

Rows can be styled using the rowClassName and rowStyle props, and their heights can be fixed or vary from row to row. The table below has sortable headers, a custom header and cell renderer, alternating row styles, optional dynamic row heights, and a placeholder that is shown when there are no rows. Dynamic row heights Hide index column Hide header No rows import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 SortDirection,
 SortIndicator,
 Table,
 type ColumnProps,
 type TableProps
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 disableHeader,
 hideIndexColumn,
 list,
 useDynamicRowHeight
}: {
 disableHeader: boolean;
 hideIndexColumn: boolean;
 list: ListItem[];
 useDynamicRowHeight: boolean;
}) {
 const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState("index");
 const [sortDirection, setSortDirection] = useState<
 TableProps["sortDirection"]
 >(SortDirection.ASC);

 const sortedList = useMemo(() => {
 const key = sortBy as keyof ListItem;
 const sorted = [...list].sort((a, b) =>
 a[key] < b[key] ? -1 : a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : 0
 );
 return sortDirection === SortDirection.DESC ? sorted.reverse() : sorted;
 }, [list, sortBy, sortDirection]);

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 disableHeader={disableHeader}
 headerClassName="normal-case"
 headerHeight={36}
 height={300}
 noRowsRenderer={() => (
 <div className="h-full flex items-center justify-center text-slate-400">
 No rows
 </div>
 )}
 overscanRowCount={10}
 rowClassName={({ index }) =>
 index < 0
 ? "border-b border-white/20 bg-white/5"
 : index % 2 === 0
 ? "border-b border-white/10"
 : "border-b border-white/10 bg-white/5"
 }
 rowCount={sortedList.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => sortedList[index]}
 rowHeight={
 useDynamicRowHeight ? ({ index }) => sortedList[index].size : 40
 }
 sort={({ sortBy, sortDirection }) => {
 setSortBy(sortBy);
 setSortDirection(sortDirection);
 }}
 sortBy={sortBy}
 sortDirection={sortDirection}
 width={width}
 >
 {/* Falsy children are ignored, so columns can be shown or hidden */}
 {!hideIndexColumn && (
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={80} />
 )}
 <Column
 dataKey="name"
 headerRenderer={fullNameHeaderRenderer}
 width={120}
 />
 <Column
 cellRenderer={({ cellData }) => (
 <span className="text-slate-300">{cellData}</span>
 )}
 className="truncate"
 dataKey="random"
 disableSort
 flexGrow={1}
 label="The description label is really long so that it will be truncated"
 width={210}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

const fullNameHeaderRenderer: ColumnProps["headerRenderer"] = ({
 dataKey,
 sortBy,
 sortDirection
}) => {
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center">
 Full Name
 {sortBy === dataKey && <SortIndicator sortDirection={sortDirection} />}
 </div>
 );
}; rowClassName (and rowStyle) can be a static value or a function of the row index. They are also applied to the header row, which is passed an index of -1. rowHeight can be a fixed number or a function of the row index. If row heights change after they have been measured, call recomputeRowHeights . Falsy children are ignored, so columns can be shown or hidden conditionally. noRowsRenderer renders placeholder content when rowCount is 0. The headerClassName prop applies a class to every column header (normal-case above overrides the default uppercase style). Sorting is covered in more detail on the sorting page.

headings.text:
SortIndicator The default header renderer shows a SortIndicator (an arrow icon) beside the label of the column the table is sorted by. If you render custom headers, you can use SortIndicator to show the sort direction yourself: import { SortIndicator, type ColumnProps } from "react-virtualized";

const headerRenderer: ColumnProps["headerRenderer"] = ({
 dataKey,
 label,
 sortBy,
 sortDirection
}) => (
 <div className="flex items-center gap-1">
 <span className="italic">{label}</span>
 {sortBy === dataKey && <SortIndicator sortDirection={sortDirection} />}
 </div>
);

text:
Table headers can be made sortable. Click a column header below to sort by it; click it again to reverse the sort direction. Table does not sort data itself. Instead, it tells you how the data should be sorted, and renders the current sort state: sort is called when a sortable header is clicked (or activated with the ENTER or SPACE key). It is passed the sortBy (the clicked column's dataKey) and the suggested sortDirection, as well as the column's defaultSortDirection and the DOM event. Sort your data accordingly, and update your state to re-render the table. sortBy is the dataKey that data is currently sorted by (if it is sorted at all). sortDirection is the direction data is currently sorted in (if it is sorted at all). import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import {
 Column,
 SortDirection,
 Table,
 type TableProps
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example() {
 const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState<TableProps["sortBy"]>("index");
 const [sortDirection, setSortDirection] = useState<
 TableProps["sortDirection"]
 >(SortDirection.ASC);

 // Table does not sort data itself; it only tells you how to sort it
 const sortedList = useMemo(
 () => sortList(list, sortBy, sortDirection),
 [sortBy, sortDirection]
 );

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Table
 headerHeight={36}
 height={250}
 rowClassName="border-b border-white/10"
 rowCount={sortedList.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => sortedList[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 sort={({ sortBy, sortDirection }) => {
 setSortBy(sortBy);
 setSortDirection(sortDirection);
 }}
 sortBy={sortBy}
 sortDirection={sortDirection}
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="index" label="Index" width={80} />
 <Column
 dataKey="name"
 // Sort descending when this column is first clicked
 defaultSortDirection={SortDirection.DESC}
 label="Name"
 width={120}
 />
 <Column
 dataKey="random"
 // This column can't be sorted
 disableSort
 flexGrow={1}
 label="Description"
 width={200}
 />
 </Table>
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
}

function sortList(
 list: ListItem[],
 sortBy: TableProps["sortBy"],
 sortDirection: TableProps["sortDirection"]
) {
 if (!sortBy) {
 return list;
 }

 const key = sortBy as keyof ListItem;
 const sorted = [...list].sort((a, b) =>
 a[key] < b[key] ? -1 : a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : 0
 );

 return sortDirection === SortDirection.DESC ? sorted.reverse() : sorted;
} Headers are only sortable if a sort function is provided. Individual columns can opt out with the disableSort prop. A column's defaultSortDirection ("ASC" by default) controls the suggested direction when it is first clicked; clicking it again suggests the opposite direction. See Column props for more.

headings.text:
Grid, Table and Masonry The scrollElement prop works the same way for a Grid. The window only scrolls a Grid vertically; if its columns are wider than its width, it still scrolls horizontally itself: import { Grid } from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 cellRenderer,
 width
}: {
 cellRenderer: CellRenderer;
 width: number;
}) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 columnCount={20}
 columnWidth={150}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={30}
 // The window scrolls the Grid vertically;
 // columns wider than the width still scroll horizontally within the Grid
 scrollElement="window"
 width={width}
 />
 );
} A Table's header row is scrolled along with its rows (it doesn't stay at the top of the window): import { Column, Table } from "react-virtualized";

function Example({ items, width }: { items: ListItem[]; width: number }) {
 return (
 <Table
 headerHeight={30}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowGetter={({ index }) => items[index]}
 rowHeight={30}
 // The header scrolls along with the page (it isn't sticky)
 scrollElement="window"
 width={width}
 >
 <Column dataKey="name" label="Name" width={200} />
 <Column dataKey="color" label="Color" width={100} flexGrow={1} />
 </Table>
 );
} For a Masonry, reset the cell positioner when the available width changes (e.g. in AutoSizer's onResize), so that the number of columns fits the new width: import { useRef } from "react";
import { Masonry, type MasonryImperativeAPI } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ cellCount }: { cellCount: number }) {
 const masonryRef = useRef<MasonryImperativeAPI>(null);

 // When the available width changes, update the number of columns
 const onResize = ({ width }: { width: number }) => {
 const columnWidth = 200;
 const spacer = 10;

 cellPositioner.reset({
 columnCount: Math.max(
 1,
 Math.floor((width + spacer) / (columnWidth + spacer))
 ),
 columnWidth,
 spacer
 });
 masonryRef.current?.recomputeCellPositions();
 };

 return (
 <AutoSizer
 onResize={onResize}
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <Masonry
 cellCount={cellCount}
 cellMeasurerCache={cache}
 cellPositioner={cellPositioner}
 cellRenderer={cellRenderer}
 ref={masonryRef}
 // The page (rather than the Masonry) scrolls
 scrollElement="window"
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} MultiGrid always scrolls itself, so it doesn't have a scrollElement prop.

Scrolling to a row A component with a scrollElement is scrolled with its regular methods: the List or Table's scrollToRow and scrollToPosition, or the Grid's scrollToCell and scrollToPosition. They scroll the scroll element (e.g. the window) instead of the component itself, so a regular ref is all that's needed. (This page is itself part of a larger layout, so the example below scrolls a container; see scrolling an element.) import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import {
 List,
 type ListImperativeAPI,
 type RowRendererParams
} from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 // This example scrolls a container (see "Scrolling an element");
 // with scrollElement="window", the List's methods scroll the window instead
 const [scrollElement, setScrollElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(
 null
 );

 // A regular ref, to call the List's methods
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div
 className="flex items-center gap-2 px-3 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 <span className="w-10 text-right text-slate-400">{index}</span>
 {items[index].name}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <div className="flex flex-col gap-2 h-full">
 <div className="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
 <button
 className="px-2 rounded bg-white/10"
 onClick={() =>
 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ align: "center", index: 500 })
 }
 >
 Scroll to row 500
 </button>
 <button
 className="px-2 rounded bg-white/10"
 onClick={() =>
 listRef.current?.scrollToRow({ align: "start", index: 0 })
 }
 >
 Scroll to the first row
 </button>
 </div>
 <div className="grow min-h-0 overflow-auto" ref={setScrollElement}>
 <div className="p-3 mb-2 rounded bg-white/10">
 Content above the list
 </div>
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollElement={scrollElement}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} Things to keep in mind: These are one-time scrolls: the row isn't kept in view if row heights change afterwards. Offsets passed to scrollToPosition (and returned by getOffsetForRow) are relative to the top of the list, not to the top of the page. To scroll to a row that is being added, call scrollToRow once the list has rendered it (e.g. in an effect). Rows that haven't been measured yet (e.g. by CellMeasurer) are positioned using their estimated heights, so the row may not end up exactly aligned. The list's position within the scroll element is measured again each time it scrolls it, so content above the list that has changed size is taken into account. A Grid is only scrolled vertically by its scroll element; if it is wider than its width, it still scrolls horizontally itself. scrollToRow scrolls immediately. For a smooth scroll, compute the row's offset on the page and scroll the window yourself: // Smoothly scrolls the window to a row of a List with scrollElement="window".
// (listElementRef is passed to the List's elementRef prop.)
function smoothScrollToRow(index: number) {
 const list = listRef.current;
 const listElement = listElementRef.current;
 if (list && listElement) {
 // Offset of the row within the List, and of the List within the page
 const rowOffset = list.getOffsetForRow({ align: "start", index });
 const listOffset = listElement.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY;

 window.scrollTo({ behavior: "smooth", top: listOffset + rowOffset });
 }
}

Content above the component The component's position within the scroll element (e.g. how far it is from the top of the page) is measured again whenever the scroll element scrolls or is resized, and whenever the component re-renders. If content above it changes size without the component re-rendering (e.g. a banner is dismissed, and the component's props don't change), re-render it with forceUpdateGrid() (List and Table) or forceUpdate() (Grid and Masonry): import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { List, type ListImperativeAPI } from "react-virtualized";

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);
 const [showBanner, setShowBanner] = useState(true);

 // The banner above the list changed size, but the List's props didn't
 // (so it doesn't re-render); re-render it to re-measure its position
 useLayoutEffect(() => {
 listRef.current?.forceUpdateGrid();
 }, [showBanner]);

 return (
 <>
 {showBanner && (
 <div onClick={() => setShowBanner(false)}>Click to dismiss</div>
 )}
 <List
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollElement="window"
 width={600}
 />
 </>
 );
} Until the component re-renders (or the scroll element scrolls), it renders the rows for its previous position, so part of the viewport may be left empty. How likely this is depends on the browser: while the page is scrolled, browsers that support scroll anchoring (e.g. Chrome and Firefox) adjust the scroll position when content above changes size, which re-measures the component; browsers without scroll anchoring (e.g. Safari) don't. The rows rendered beyond the viewport (see the overscanRowCount prop) hide small changes, but not content above the component growing. So call forceUpdateGrid() or forceUpdate() whenever content above the component may change size.

Migrating from WindowScroller In version 10, the WindowScroller component has been replaced by the scrollElement prop. Instead of wrapping the component in a WindowScroller and wiring its render parameters to the autoHeight, height, isScrolling, onScroll and scrollTop props (all of which have been removed, except height and onScroll), pass scrollElement directly: // react-virtualized 9.x
function Example() {
 return (
 <WindowScroller>
 {({ height, isScrolling, onChildScroll, registerChild, scrollTop }) => (
 <div ref={registerChild}>
 <List
 autoHeight
 height={height}
 isScrolling={isScrolling}
 onScroll={onChildScroll}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollTop={scrollTop}
 width={width}
 />
 </div>
 )}
 </WindowScroller>
 );
} // react-virtualized 10
import { List } from "react-virtualized";

function Example() {
 return (
 <List
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollElement="window"
 width={width}
 />
 );
} What replaced each part of the WindowScroller API: registerChild (and the wrapper element it was attached to): not needed; the component measures its position from its own element. Use a regular ref to call its methods. height (and width): the viewport height is measured by the component itself. Use AutoSizer for the width (the window's width usually isn't the width available to the component). scrollTop, isScrolling and onChildScroll: handled by the component. isScrolling is still passed to the rowRenderer (or cellRenderer). scrollLeft: horizontal window scrolling is no longer supported. The scrollElement prop: moved to the component, and accepts "window" (instead of the window object) or an element. updatePosition(): not needed; the position is measured again on every scroll, on resize, and whenever the component re-renders (see "Content above the component" above). updateScrollTopOnUpdatePosition is no longer needed either. onScroll: use the component's own onScroll prop, which reports how far the scroll element has been scrolled past the top of the component. onResize: use a resize event listener on the window (or a ResizeObserver on the scroll element). serverHeight and serverWidth: pass height (used until the scroll element has been measured) and width to the component. scrollingResetTimeInterval: use the scrollingResetTimeInterval prop of the Grid, List or Masonry. IS_SCROLLING_TIMEOUT and defaultWindowScrollerProps: removed.

text:
A List, Grid, Table or Masonry can be scrolled by the window (rather than having a scrollbar of its own) by setting its scrollElement prop to "window". This can be used to create layouts similar to Facebook or Twitter news feeds, where the list scrolls along with the rest of the page. With a scroll element, the component: renders at its full height (it doesn't scroll itself vertically), renders only the rows (or cells) visible within the window's viewport, and its scroll methods (scrollToRow, scrollToCell and scrollToPosition) scroll the window; see scrolling to a row. A regular ref can be used to call its methods. Only the width needs to be provided, e.g. by AutoSizer: import { useRef } from "react";
import { List, type ListImperativeAPI } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({
 rowCount,
 rowRenderer
}: {
 rowCount: number;
 rowRenderer: RowRenderer;
}) {
 // A regular ref; the List's scroll methods scroll the window
 const listRef = useRef<ListImperativeAPI>(null);

 return (
 // Only the width is measured; the height is that of the window
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 ref={listRef}
 rowCount={rowCount}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollElement="window"
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 );
} The window is the most common scroll element, but any scrollable element can be used instead; see scrolling an element (which also has a live example).

Instead of "window", the scrollElement prop can be a scrollable element (e.g. a container with overflow: auto that the component is rendered within, along with other content). The component then scrolls along with the rest of that element's content, and renders only the rows visible within the element. The component needs the element itself (not a ref object), so store it in state with a ref callback; this re-renders the component once the element has been mounted. Until then, the scrollElement is null, and the component renders its top rows (at its full height). In the example below, the content above the list and the list itself share a single scrollbar (the container's); the list doesn't have a scrollbar of its own. import { useState } from "react";
import { List, type RowRendererParams } from "react-virtualized";
import { AutoSizer } from "react-virtualized-auto-sizer";

function Example({ items }: { items: ListItem[] }) {
 // The List needs the scrollable element itself,
 // so store it in state (rather than a ref) to re-render once it's mounted;
 // until then it's null, and the List renders its first rows
 const [scrollElement, setScrollElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(
 null
 );

 const rowRenderer = ({ index, key, style }: RowRendererParams) => (
 <div
 className="flex items-center px-3 border-b border-white/10"
 key={key}
 style={style}
 >
 {items[index].name}
 </div>
 );

 return (
 <div className="h-full overflow-auto" ref={setScrollElement}>
 <div className="p-3 mb-2 rounded bg-white/10">Content above the list</div>
 <AutoSizer
 renderProp={({ width }) =>
 width !== undefined ? (
 <List
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={30}
 rowRenderer={rowRenderer}
 scrollElement={scrollElement}
 width={width}
 />
 ) : null
 }
 />
 </div>
 );
} Size changes of the scroll element are detected with a ResizeObserver, and the component's position within it is measured again whenever it scrolls.
